{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788280525352,"sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bcfeb79c57f21ffb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\ndef load(f):\n    with open(f, encoding='utf-8') as fh:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(fh))\n\n# Season ranges\nb = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nn = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nc = load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nl = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nbr = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\n\nprint('Brasileirao seasons:', sorted(Counter(r['season'] for r in b).items()))\nprint('novo seasons:', sorted(Counter(r['Ano'] for r in n).items()))\nprint('Copa seasons:', min(int(r['season']) for r in c), '-', max(int(r['season']) for r in c))\nprint('Libert seasons:', min(int(r['season']) for r in l), '-', max(int(r['season']) for r in l))\nprint('BR-Football tournaments:', Counter(r['tournament'] for r in br))\nby_t_s = defaultdict(Counter)\nfor r in br: by_t_s[r['tournament']][r['date'][:4]] += 1\nfor t, c_ in by_t_s.items(): print(' ', t, sorted(c_.items()))\nEOF","workdir":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d"},"output":"Brasileirao seasons: [('2012', 380), ('2013', 380), ('2014', 380), ('2015', 380), ('2016', 380), ('2017', 380), ('2018', 380), ('2019', 380), ('2020', 380), ('2021', 380), ('2022', 380)]\nnovo seasons: [('2003', 552), ('2004', 552), ('2005', 462), ('2006', 380), ('2007', 380), ('2008', 380), ('2009', 380), ('2010', 380), ('2011', 380), ('2012', 380), ('2013', 380), ('2014', 380), ('2015', 380), ('2016', 380), ('2017', 380), ('2018', 380), ('2019', 380)]\nCopa seasons: 2012 - 2021\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 18, in <module>\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 18, in <genexpr>\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'NA'\n","metadata":{"output":"Brasileirao seasons: [('2012', 380), ('2013', 380), ('2014', 380), ('2015', 380), ('2016', 380), ('2017', 380), ('2018', 380), ('2019', 380), ('2020', 380), ('2021', 380), ('2022', 380)]\nnovo seasons: [('2003', 552), ('2004', 552), ('2005', 462), ('2006', 380), ('2007', 380), ('2008', 380), ('2009', 380), ('2010', 380), ('2011', 380), ('2012', 380), ('2013', 380), ('2014', 380), ('2015', 380), ('2016', 380), ('2017', 380), ('2018', 380), ('2019', 380)]\nCopa seasons: 2012 - 2021\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 18, in <module>\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 18, in <genexpr>\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'NA'\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\ndef load(f):\n    with open(f, encoding='utf-8') as fh:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(fh))\n\n# Season ranges\nb = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nn = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nc = load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nl = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nbr = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\n\nprint('Brasileirao seasons:', sorted(Counter(r['season'] for r in b).items()))\nprint('novo seasons:', sorted(Counter(r['Ano'] for r in n).items()))\nprint('Copa seasons:', min(int(r['season']) for r in c), '-', max(int(r['season']) for r in c))\nprint('Libert seasons:', min(int(r['season']) for r in l), '-', max(int(r['season']) for r in l))\nprint('BR-Football tournaments:', Counter(r['tournament'] for r in br))\nby_t_s = defaultdict(Counter)\nfor r in br: by_t_s[r['tournament']][r['date'][:4]] += 1\nfor t, c_ in by_t_s.items(): print(' ', t, sorted(c_.items()))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788280525346,"end":1788280525350}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting. The FIFA dataset seems to be FIFA 19 (2018-ish), and contains only a subset of Brazilian clubs (each with exactly 20 players — looks like the dataset author only included some Brazilian clubs fully). Flamengo and Palmeiras are NOT in this FIFA dataset apparently. Let me double check with a broader search... Actually the earlier grep for 'flamengo' in club names returned nothing, so Flamengo is not there. That's fine — the spec's example questions like \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" might return empty; but the spec says \"Filter FIFA data by Club containing 'Flamengo'\". The behavior is to filter; if no results, return no results. But wait — the spec example shows Flamengo: 8 players. That's just illustrative (\"Example answer format\"). The data is what it is. Hmm, but let me check 'Paraná', 'Atlético Paranaense', 'Atlético Mineiro', 'América FC (Minas Gerais)' — FIFA club naming uses full names.\n\nActually wait — let me check if there are more Brazilian clubs beyond the top-25 shown. Let me look for clubs where nationality Brazil count > 0, all of them, plus check \"Palmeiras\"/\"Corinthians\"/\"São Paulo\" aren't there. Also check \"Goiás\", \"Vasco\", \"Athletico\".\n\nAnyway, for player queries I need a club-name normalization that maps FIFA club names to canonical Brazilian club names:\n- 'Atlético Mineiro' -> Atlético-MG\n- 'Atlético Paranaense' -> Athletico-PR (same club, renamed)\n- 'América FC (Minas Gerais)' -> América-MG\n- 'Sport Club do Recife' -> Sport-PE\n- 'Ceará Sporting Club' -> Ceará-CE\n- 'Paraná' -> Paraná (clube)\n- 'Grêmio' -> Grêmio\n- 'Santos' -> Santos\n- 'Vitória' -> Vitória-BA\n- 'Bahia' -> Bahia-BA\n- 'Chapecoense' -> Chapecoense-SC\n- 'Botafogo' -> Botafogo-RJ (or Botafogo-PB? ambiguity — in Serie A context it's Botafogo-RJ)\n- 'Fluminense' -> Fluminense-RJ\n- 'Cruzeiro' -> Cruzeiro-MG\n- 'Internacional' -> Internacional-RS\n\nThis mapping approach: a curated alias table mapping normalized keys -> canonical names. That's the cleanest way to satisfy \"Handles team name variations correctly\".\n\nNow let me design the whole system:\n\n### Architecture\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n├── __init__.py\n├── models.py          # dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamRecord/Standings\n├── normalize.py       # team name normalization, alias tables, state suffix stripping\n├── loader.py          # load all 6 CSVs into normalized Match/Player objects\n├── service.py         # SoccerDataService: query + stats API (pure functions over data)\n├── server.py          # MCP server (mcp 2.x MCPServer) exposing tools\n└── __main__.py        # python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp\ntests/\n├── test_bdd_match_queries.py   # Gherkin-style BDD tests (Given/When/Then comments + pytest)\n├── test_bdd_team_queries.py\n├── test_bdd_player_queries.py\n├── test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n├── test_bdd_statistics.py\n└── test_mcp_server.py  # verify tools registered & callable via in-memory client\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml?        # maybe minimal, or just requirements.txt\n```\n\nActually, the venv already has pytest installed; I installed `mcp`. I should write requirements.txt with mcp and pytest.\n\nNote: venv already existed with pytest; the environment might reset... but no, my job: make sure tests pass in this venv. I'll also make the MCP server importable with graceful fallback if `mcp` isn't installed? No — keep it simple, mcp is installed.\n\n### Data model\n\nMatch dataclass:\n- date: datetime.date\n- time: str | None\n- competition: str (normalized: \"Brasileirão Série A\", \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Copa Libertadores\", ...)\n- source_file / source dataset id\n- home_team: canonical team name\n- away_team: canonical team name\n- home_goals: int | None\n- away_goals: int | None\n- round: str (\"Round 22\", \"Final\", \"Group Stage\", ...)\n- stage: str | None\n- season: int\n- stadium: str | None\n- extra stats: corners, shots, attacks (from BR-Football)\n\nCompetition normalization:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv -> \"Brasileirão Série A\" (2012-2023)\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv -> \"Brasileirão Série A\" (2003-2019) — same competition, historical\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv -> \"Copa do Brasil\"\n- Libertadores_Matches.csv -> \"Copa Libertadores\"\n- BR-Football-Dataset.csv -> tournament column: \"Serie A\" -> Brasileirão Série A, \"Serie B\" -> Brasileirão Série B, \"Serie C\" -> Série C, \"Copa do Brasil\" -> Copa do Brasil\n\nWait — if I merge Brasileirao_Matches and novo_campeonato (2003-2019 overlap with 2012-2019), there will be duplicate matches for the same fixture. Overlapping seasons 2012-2019 appear in both files. E.g., a 2019 Flamengo vs Coritiba match would appear in both. For head-to-head queries, dedup matters.\n\nDedup approach: build a dedup key of (competition, season, date, home_team, away_team) and keep first occurrence, preferring the richer record. But note: the same fixture can legitimately appear twice in a season (home and away legs are different dates, so fine). Replays in Copa do Brasil? Different dates too. Two-legged ties: different dates. Same-date duplicates are essentially the same match across files.\n\nBut careful: BR-Football-Dataset and Brazilian_Cup_Matches both cover Copa do Brasil with different team naming (\"Sao Paulo\" vs \"São Paulo - SP\"?) — after canonicalization they should converge. Let me check: Brazilian_Cup: \"América - MG\", BR-Football: \"America MG\"? Hmm, need to check accents. BR-Football sample showed \"America MG\", \"Athletic Club MG\". So canonicalization: strip accents, remove state suffix, and alias map.\n\nA generic canonical function:\n1. Strip accents (NFKD -> ASCII)\n2. Lowercase\n3. Remove content in parentheses (country tags like \"(URU)\", \"(Minas Gerais)\")\n4. Remove state suffix: patterns like \"-XX\" at end, \" - XX\" at end, where XX in Brazilian UFs\n5. Remove punctuation, collapse spaces\n6. Apply alias table mapping normalized variants to canonical name\n\nThe alias table handles:\n- \"atletico paranaense\" / \"athletico paranaense\" / \"athletico pr\" / \"atletico pr\" -> \"Athletico-PR\" canonical. Hmm wait — historically Atlético-PR was renamed Athletico-PR in 2019. For matching purposes, treat as same club with canonical \"Athletico-PR\".\n- \"atletico mineiro\" / \"atletico mg\" -> \"Atlético-MG\"\n- \"amERICA fc (minas gerais)\" -> after paren removal \"america fc\" — hmm that's ambiguous with América-RN. FIFA 'América FC (Minas Gerais)' — after removing parens: \"america fc\" which maps ambiguously. So alias must include the paren content: normalized \"america fc minas gerais\" -> \"América-MG\". So I should normalize the full string including parens for alias lookups, but ALSO try without parens. Order of operations:\n   1. accent-strip, lowercase, remove dots/hyphens -> spaces\n   2. alias lookup on full normalized string (e.g. \"america fc minas gerais\" -> \"America-MG\")\n   3. if no alias, remove parenthesized parts and state suffix, then alias lookup again\n   4. fallback: use cleaned name as canonical\n\n- \"sport club do recife\" / \"sport recife\" / \"sport pe\" / \"sport\" -> \"Sport-PE\" (hmm \"Sport\" alone is Sport Recife in Brazil context)\n- \"ceara sporting club\" / \"ceara\" -> \"Ceará-SC\"? Canonical \"Ceará-CE\"\n- \"sao paulo\" / \"sao paulo fc\" / \"sao paulo sp\" -> \"São Paulo\"\n- \"corinthians\" / \"sport club corinthians paulista\" -> \"Corinthians\"\n- \"palmeiras\" / \"sociiedade esportiva palmeiras\" -> \"Palmeiras\"\n- \"gremio\" / \"gremio rs\" -> \"Grêmio\"\n- \"vasco\" / \"vasco da gama\" / \"vasco rj\" -> \"Vasco da Gama\"\n- \"botafogo\" / \"botafogo rj\" / \"botafogo pb\" -> careful: \"Botafogo-RJ\" vs \"Botafogo-PB\" are different clubs! State suffix matters for disambiguation. If suffix present, keep it: \"botafogo pb\" -> \"Botafogo-PB\", \"botafogo rj\" -> \"Botafogo-RJ\", bare \"botafogo\" -> \"Botafogo-RJ\" (the famous one).\n- \"flamengo\" / \"flamengo rj\" -> \"Flamengo\"\n- \"internacional\" / \"internacional rs\" -> \"Internacional\"\n- \"goias\" -> \"Goiás\"\n- \"bahia\" / \"esporte clube bahia\" / \"bahia ba\" -> \"Bahia\"\n- \"vitoria\" / \"vitoria ba\" vs \"vitoria es\" — ambiguous! FIFA 'Vitória' — in FIFA 19 that's Vitória-BA (Série A). Bare \"vitoria\" -> \"Vitória-BA\".\n- \"america mg\" / \"america fc minas gerais\" -> \"América-MG\"; \"america rn\" -> \"América-RN\"; bare \"america\" -> ambiguous... in Série A context (novo file 2003-2019) \"América-MG\"/\"América-RN\" both appear WITH suffixes so fine. BR-Football has \"America MG\", \"America RN\" with suffixes. Bare \"America\" — maybe América-SP (from 2007 Série C?) not relevant much. I'll map bare \"america\" -> \"América-MG\"? Risky. Let me check which bare \"america\" forms actually appear in the data.\n\nLet me be systematic: I'll write a script to extract all team name variants from all match files and FIFA clubs, normalize them, and look for collisions/ambiguities. Then build the alias table to cover the major ones. I don't need to canonicalize every single minor club perfectly — the important thing is that (a) the same club from different files maps to the same canonical name when possible, (b) queries by user-facing names (Flamengo, Palmeiras...) find matches.\n\nActually, a more robust approach for cross-file matching: build canonical names primarily via normalization (strip suffix/accents), and use alias table only for known variants. When two normalized forms differ only by state suffix, keep them distinct but ALSO index by base name for queries. For head-to-head and standings, exact canonical matching matters. E.g. \"Botafogo-RJ\" (Brasileirao file) vs \"Botafogo\" (novo file, bare?) — let me check: novo sample shows 'Botafogo-RJ' with suffix. BR-Football shows... let me check whether BR-Football uses \"Botafogo\" bare or \"Botafogo RJ\". The sample showed 'America MG' style (\"America MG\" with space-separated state?). Actually sample shows 'America MG', 'America RN', 'ASA AL', 'Aguia Negra-MS', 'Athletic Club MG', 'Athletico Paranaense' (spelled out, no suffix!). So BR-Football mixes: some with state (\"America MG\"), some spelled-out (\"Athletico Paranaense\"), some bare (\"Altos\", \"Amazonas\", \"ABC\").\n\nOK here's my plan for canonicalization with state handling:\n- Detect and strip trailing state tokens: last token in {two-letter UF} preceded by separator. UFs: AC, AL, AM, AP, BA, CE, DF, ES, GO, MA, MG, MS, MT, PA, PB, PE, PI, PR, RJ, RN, RO, RR, RS, SC, SE, SP, TO. Also country tags for Libertadores: (URU), (EQU), (ARG), etc — keep those distinct! In Libertadores, \"Nacional (URU)\" and \"Barcelona-EQU\" are foreign clubs. If I strip \"(URU)\" I get \"Nacional\" (Uruguayan club) — no conflict with Brazilian clubs named Nacional? There might be \"Nacional-AM\" or \"Nacional-SP\"... For Libertadores queries, keeping \"(URU)\" suffix in canonical name is actually informative. Hmm. But for h2h between Brazilian teams in Libertadores, both sides come from the same file mostly, so consistent naming within file. I'll preserve foreign-club country tags as part of canonical name (e.g., \"Nacional (URU)\").\n\nSo canonicalization rules:\n1. Unicode normalize NFKD, drop combining marks (accents).\n2. Lowercase.\n3. Alias table lookup (full-string, punctuation-insensitive) — handles FIFA full names like \"sport club do recife\", \"america fc minas gerais\", \"atletico paranaense\".\n4. Otherwise: extract trailing state suffix if present (patterns: \"-sp\", \" sp\", \" - sp\" as last token where token in UF set or 3-letter country codes?) — careful with \"Barcelona-EQU\" (3-letter country code EQU = Ecuador). Hmm, in Libertadores: \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Nacional (URU)\". Let me check Libertadores team names more.\n\nLet me check what suffixes appear in Libertadores. Also Copa do Brasil uses \" - RJ\" pattern.\n\nThen:\n5. Remove corporate suffixes? No, keep.\n6. Canonical display name: I'll reconstruct from the base name with nice capitalization... Actually simpler: canonical = the alias table entry if found; else use title-cased base name + state suffix if present (e.g., \"Flamengo\" + \"RJ\" -> \"Flamengo-RJ\"? or without?).\n\nHmm wait. There's a subtlety: For h2h \"Flamengo vs Fluminense\", matches appear in Brasileirao_Matches as \"Flamengo-RJ\"/\"Fluminense-RJ\", in novo as \"Flamengo\"/\"Fluminense\" (bare), in BR-Football as \"Flamengo\"/\"Fluminense\". If canonical keeps state suffix for some files and not others, matching breaks. So canonical name should DROP state suffixes for major clubs (i.e., \"Flamengo-RJ\" -> \"Flamengo\"). But for genuinely ambiguous bases (Botafogo-RJ vs Botafogo-PB, America-RN vs America-MG vs America-SP, Vitoria-BA vs Vitoria-ES, Atletico-MG vs Atletico-GO vs Atletico-PR...) I need state suffix retained for disambiguation!\n\nResolution: two-tier canonical identity:\n- `club_id`: unique internal key — normalized base + state suffix when present (e.g., \"botafogo_rj\", \"america_mg\", \"flamengo\"). This is unique per club.\n- Alias table maps known variants to club_ids: \"botafogo\" -> \"botafogo_rj\" (bare defaults to famous club), \"atletico paranaense\" -> \"atletico_pr\", etc.\n- For clubs whose base name is unambiguous within the dataset, base-only id works: \"flamengo_rj\"? Hmm, but if Flamengo always appears as \"Flamengo-RJ\" or \"Flamengo\", I could make club_id = \"flamengo\" (dropping state) — but then \"vitoria\" would conflate Vitória-BA and Vitória-ES.\n\nSimplest consistent scheme: club_id ALWAYS = base + state when state is known; alias table maps bare base names to the \"default\" club (most famous) for query purposes. And alias table maps FIFA full names to (base, state). Within data files, if a team appears bare without state in one file and with state in another, I need per-file defaults... e.g. BR-Football \"Vitoria\" — which Vitória? BR-Football covers Serie A/B/C 2012-2023? Vitória-BA is the Série A one. There's also \"Vitória-ES\"? Rarely in national série A/B. FIFA 'Vitória' = Vitória-BA (Série A 2018). novo file: 'Vitória' bare? Sample showed 'Vitória' — let me check if novo uses suffixes consistently: sample shows 'América-MG', 'Athletico-PR', 'Atlético-GO', 'Botafogo-RJ' — with suffixes. And 'Flamengo', 'Corinthians', 'Grêmio', 'Cruzeiro', 'Bahia', 'Ceará', 'Chapecoense', 'CSA' — bare! So novo mixes: some with suffix, some bare. So per-club the file is consistent (Flamengo always bare in novo, América always suffixed).\n\nSo my approach: a curated alias/normalization table where canonical club key for ambiguous bases REQUIRES state; for query lookups of bare ambiguous names, pick default. And in files where ambiguous base appears bare... e.g., does BR-Football have bare \"Vitoria\"? It has \"Vitória-BA\"? BR-Football is unaccented: \"Vitoria BA\"? Let me check. I'll write an exploration script.\n\nGiven time constraints, let me now decide the pragmatic approach used by many implementations:\n\n**Canonical key** = normalized string after:\n- accent strip, lowercase\n- replace punctuation with spaces, collapse\n- strip trailing state/country tag tokens (from a known list) — but ONLY if remaining base is non-empty\n- map through ALIAS dict (both pre-suffix-strip and post-suffix-strip forms), where ALIAS includes:\n  - \"atletico paranaense\" -> \"athletico-pr\", \"atletico pr\" -> \"athletico-pr\", \"athletico pr\" -> \"athletico-pr\", \"athletico paranaense\"-> \"athletico-pr\"\n  - \"sport club do recife\" -> \"sport-pe\", \"sport recife\" -> \"sport-pe\", \"sport pe\" -> \"sport-pe\", \"sport\" -> \"sport-pe\"\n  - \"ceara sporting club\" -> \"ceara\", ...\n  - \"america fc minas gerais\" -> \"america-mg\"\n  - \"vasco da gama\" -> \"vasco\"\n  - etc.\n\nWait, but there's the \"athletico pr\" vs \"atletico pr\" issue: Brasileirao file (2012-2023) uses \"Atletico-PR\" unaccented for pre-2019 and presumably \"Athletico-PR\" after rename? Let me check. And novo uses \"Athletico-PR\". These two spellings refer to the same club historically. Similarly \"Atletico-GO\" vs \"Atlético-GO\" — accent strip handles that. But \"Athletico\" vs \"Atletico\" — h vs no h — same club (renamed 2019). I must unify them or h2h/standings split. ALIAS: \"athletico pr\" -> \"atletico-pr\"? or canonical \"athletico-pr\"? Either way, unify.\n\nSimilarly \"Avai\" vs \"Avaí\" — accent strip. \"Criciuma\" vs \"Criciúma\" — accent strip. \"Gremio\" vs \"Grêmio\" — accent strip. \"Goias\" vs \"Goiás\" — accent. \"Ceara\" vs \"Ceará\" — accent. \"Coritiba\" fine. \"Parana\" (FIFA club 'Paraná') vs \"parana\"? FIFA 'Paraná' is Paraná Clube (PR). BR-Football might have \"Parana\"? Both -> \"parana\". Fine.\n\nOK. Display names: I'll keep a DISPLAY dict for pretty names of major clubs (with accents: \"São Paulo\", \"Grêmio\", \"Athletico-PR\"...). For unknown clubs, generate title-case display from the key.\n\n**Now the h2h example**: \"Flamengo vs Fluminense\" — both appear in 4 files (Brasileirao 2012-2023, novo 2003-2019, BR-Football Serie A, plus Libertadores/Copa do Brasil). Overlapping seasons between Brasileirao and novo (2012-2019) will duplicate fixtures. BR-Football Serie A covers 2012-2023 too? Let me check BR-Football date range and whether Serie A matches there duplicate Brasileirao_Matches. BR-Football sample dates: 2023-09-24 Serie... it says tournament 'Serie A'. If BR-Football Serie A 2012-2023 duplicates Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2023 exactly, dedup is essential to avoid double-counting.\n\nDedup strategy: key = (competition, season, canonical_home, canonical_away, date, home_goals, away_goals). Keep the richest record (one with more stats). Actually key on (competition, season, date, home, away) — two different results for same fixture+date would be data conflicts, take first. Hmm, but two-legged Copa do Brasil finals are different dates, fine. What about BR-Football having BOTH legs of a tie on... no, different dates.\n\nEdge: Libertadores group stage — same fixture once. Fine.\n\nWhat about 2020+ Brasileirao in BR-Football but not in Brasileirao_Matches? Brasileirao_Matches seasons 2012-2023? Let me check season ranges per file. I'll write an exploration script.\n\n**Standings computation**: For Brasileirão Série A seasons, compute standings from matches (3 pts win). Which file to use for a given season? Prefer: Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2023), novo (2003-2011 only after dedup vs Brasileirao_Matches... actually novo covers 2003-2019; Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2023?). After dedup by (competition, season, date, home, away), duplicates collapse; season match counts should then be ~380 per season. Let me verify counts: 4180 matches / 12 seasons (2012-2023) = 348/season average; 2003-2019 novo = 6886/17 = 405/season. Combined dedup: 2003-2023 Série A. Good.\n\nBut wait — dedup by date could fail if the two files record different dates for the same fixture (unlikely — official dates). Also team naming: novo \"Flamengo\" vs Brasileirao \"Flamengo-RJ\" — canonical both \"flamengo\" (strip state). Good. But \"Atletico-PR\" (Brasileirao) vs \"Athletico-PR\" (novo) — alias unifies to \"athletico-pr\" hmm wait which canonical? Let me use canonical \"athletico-pr\" for both. Wait, actually — hmm, Atlético-PR renamed to Athletico-PR in 2019. But it's the same club. For query purposes unify. I'll canonicalize both \"atletico pr\" and \"athletico pr\" -> \"athletico-pr\". Hmm, actually for display, \"Athletico-PR\" is the current name. OK.\n\nAlso \"Cuiaba-MT\" vs \"Cuiabá-MT\" — accent strip. \"Bahia-BA\" vs \"Bahia\" — strip state.\n\nNow — an important question: should canonical names keep state suffix? Consider \"America-MG\" (novo) and \"America MG\" (BR-Football) and \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" (FIFA). Canonical: strip state -> \"america\"... but America-RN and America-SP also exist! In BR-Football: \"America RN\" exists. So bare \"america\" can't be canonical for all. If I strip states from \"america mg\" -> \"america\" and \"america rn\" -> \"america\", they collide into one club — wrong.\n\nSo: canonical key KEEPS state suffix when present: \"america-mg\", \"america-rn\", \"botafogo-rj\", \"botafogo-pb\", \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico-go\", \"flamengo-rj\"?? Hmm but then \"flamengo\" (novo, bare) != \"flamengo-rj\" (Brasileirao). Unless alias maps \"flamengo\" -> \"flamengo-rj\".\n\nThe alias table must therefore include bare->suffixed defaults for ALL major clubs that appear bare in some file:\n- flamengo -> flamengo-rj\n- fluminense -> fluminense-rj\n- corinthians -> corinthians-sp\n- palmeiras -> palmeiras-sp\n- saopaulo -> sao-paulo-sp (careful: \"sao paulo\" normalized \"sao paulo\"; canonical \"sao-paulo-sp\"? hmm)\n- santos -> santos-sp\n- gremio -> gremio-rs\n- internacional -> internacional-rs\n- cruzeiro -> cruzeiro-mg\n- vasco -> vasco-rj\n- bahia -> bahia-ba\n- sport -> sport-pe\n- vitoria -> vitoria-ba\n- ceara -> ceara-ce\n- fortaleza -> fortaleza-ce\n- goias -> goias-go\n- coritiba -> coritiba-pr\n- athletico/atletico pr -> athletico-pr\n- atletico mg -> atletico-mg\n- atletico go -> atletico-go\n- chapecoense -> chapecoense-sc\n- criciuma -> criciuma-sc\n- avai -> avai-sc\n- figueirense -> figueirense-sc\n- juventude -> juventude-rs\n- nautico -> nautico-pe\n- cs a / csa -> csa-al\n- cuiaba -> cuiaba-mt\n- parana -> parana-clube? BR-Football may have \"Parana\"? both -> \"parana\" (club Paraná Clube, state PR). If one file writes \"Parana-PR\"... alias \"parana\" -> \"parana-pr\"? Hmm. I'll check data.\n\nAnd fifa club aliases:\n- \"atletico mineiro\" -> atletico-mg\n- \"atletico paranaense\"/\"athletico paranaense\" -> athletico-pr\n- \"america fc minas gerais\" -> america-mg\n- \"sport club do recife\" -> sport-pe\n- \"ceara sporting club\" -> ceara-ce\n- \"gremio\" -> gremio-rs (already)\n- \"santos\" -> santos-sp\n- \"bahia\" -> bahia-ba\n- \"botafogo\" -> botafogo-rj\n- \"fluminense\" -> fluminense-rj\n- \"cruzeiro\" -> cruzeiro-mg\n- \"internacional\" -> internacional-rs\n- \"vitoria\" -> vitoria-ba\n- \"parana\" -> parana\n- \"chapecoense\" -> chapecoense-sc\n\nThis is workable! The canonical ID includes state; display names have accents. The alias table can be generated programmatically from a table of major clubs: canonical_id -> list of alias strings (normalized).\n\nFor clubs not in the table: canonical = normalized base + (state suffix if present, kept as \"-xx\"). E.g. \"Aparecidense GO\" -> \"aparecidense-go\"; \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" — ugh, parenthetical junk. Canonical would be \"boavista sport club-rj\"? That's from Copa do Brasil file. It's fine — nobody will query it by exact name; normalization for queries will handle the common cases. I could strip parentheticals for the base. \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" -> remove parenthetical content entirely for canonical key. And \"(URU)\" country tags — treat as country marker: keep? If I strip ALL parentheticals, \"Nacional (URU)\" -> \"nacional\". There's also \"Nacional-AM\"? and \"Nacional-SP\" possibly in Copa do Brasil. Collisions possible but rare. For Libertadores, foreign clubs: keep the country tag in the canonical id to disambiguate: \"Nacional (URU)\" -> \"nacional-uru\"? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at Libertadores team name patterns first. And Copa do Brasil patterns. Then finalize.\n\nActually, let me simplify: for parenthesized country codes in Libertadores like \"(URU)\", \"(EQU)\", \"(ARG)\", \"(COL)\", \"(CHI)\", \"(PAR)\", \"(PER)\", \"(BOL)\", \"(VEN)\", \"(MEX)\", \"(ECU)\" — I'll treat a trailing \"-XXX\" or \"(XXX)\" as a country tag and preserve it in the canonical id as \"-xxx\" suffix. So \"Barcelona-EQU\" -> base \"barcelona\", tag \"equ\" -> \"barcelona-equ\". \"Nacional (URU)\" -> \"nacional-uru\". Brazilian clubs in Libertadores appear... bare? like \"Flamengo\", \"Palmeiras-SP\"? Let me check.\n\nOK, time to write an exploration script. Let me gather:\n1. Season ranges and match counts per file\n2. Team name patterns in Libertadores\n3. Whether BR-Football Serie A duplicates Brasileirao_Matches\n4. Bare ambiguous names (vitoria, america, botafogo, atletico...) across files\n\nThen design and implement. Let me also think about the MCP server tools:\n\n**MCP Tools** (matching required capabilities):\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, venue=\"any\", competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage/round=None, limit=20)` — returns list of matches with date, teams, score, competition, round/stage.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` — all matches + W/D/L summary for each side.\n3. `team_record(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\")` — matches, wins, draws, losses, GF, GA, win rate.\n4. `team_goals(team, season, competition)` — covered by team_record; maybe skip.\n5. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)` — player lookups sorted by overall desc.\n6. `club_players(club)` — covered by search_players(club=...).\n7. `standings(season, competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\")` — computed table with points, W/D/L, GF, GA, champion flagged; also relegated (bottom 4).\n8. `competition_seasons(competition)` — available seasons.\n9. `derbies(season=None)` — list famous derbies (Fla-Flu, Grenal, Paulista derbies etc.) — needs a derby table: pairs of canonical team ids with name. The spec: \"Show me all derbies in 2023\" — find matches between traditional rivals.\n10. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` — largest goal margins.\n11. `average_goals(competition=None, season=None)` — goals per match, home win rate, etc.\n12. `team_profile(team)` — competitions participated, seasons, total record — cross-file.\n13. `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)` — most recent meeting + score. (\"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians?\")\n14. `compare_teams(team_a, team_b)` — overall records + h2h.\n\nThat covers all the categories: match queries, team queries, player queries, competition queries, statistical analysis.\n\nAlso resources? MCP resources could expose datasets. Keep to tools; maybe add a couple of resources (list datasets). Keep simple but complete.\n\n**Server**: use `MCPServer` from mcp 2.x with `@server.tool()` decorator? Let me check the 2.x API — `mcp.server.mcpserver.MCPServer` with `.tool()` decorator and `.run()` for stdio. Let me introspect the signature of `tool()`.\n\n**Tests**: BDD GWT-style with pytest. The prompt says \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\" (from prompts.txt sample — though that's just a sample prompt; TASK.md itself says \"Use BDD test scenarios\" with Gherkin examples). I'll write tests structured Given/When/Then — docstrings containing Gherkin, test functions named test_*, with arrange (Given), act (When), assert (Then). Use module-scoped fixtures for data loading.\n\nAlso test the MCP server: instantiate MCPServer, call tools via `await server.call_tool(...)` or use in-memory client session. Simpler: test that the tool functions work and that the server lists tools. There's likely a way to create an in-memory client via `mcp.ClientSession` and `create_connected_server` or similar in 2.x. Let me check what's available. Alternatively test `server.list_tools()` and `server.call_tool()` directly (async). pytest with asyncio: need pytest-asyncio? Not installed. I can run async code with `asyncio.run()` inside sync test functions — simple, no extra deps.\n\nPerformance: loading 42k rows CSV at startup — fast (<1s). 18207 players. Fine.\n\nLet me now explore the data properly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dd34bb30012EIjHeNrmSQky6","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05dd25d8c001rEO1yLUtzobBUz"}}
