{"type":"text","timestamp":1788280634328,"sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","part":{"id":"prt_05dd4f82e001FWL07f9768G73b","messageID":"msg_05dd4a6dd001WAVoz0S3x8TcCf","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","type":"text","text":"BR-Football has COVID-era January/February matches belonging to the previous season — I'll handle season attribution accordingly. Now let me check the MCP 2.x server API indetail.","time":{"start":1788280633390,"end":1788280634327}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788280634851,"sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b1f758f8e7ffa5bb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"source venv/bin/activate && python << 'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint('--- tool decorator ---')\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.tool))\nprint(inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.tool)[:2000])\nprint()\nprint('--- call_tool ---')\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint(inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.call_tool)[:800])\nprint()\nprint('--- run ---')\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.run))\nEOF","workdir":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d"},"output":"--- tool decorator ---\n(self, name: 'str | None' = None, title: 'str | None' = None, description: 'str | None' = None, annotations: 'ToolAnnotations | None' = None, icons: 'list[Icon] | None' = None, meta: 'dict[str, Any] | None' = None, structured_output: 'bool | None' = None) -> 'Callable[[_CallableT], _CallableT]'\nDecorator to register a tool.\n\nTools can optionally request a Context object by adding a parameter with the\nContext type annotation. The context provides access to MCP capabilities like\nlogging, progress reporting, and resource access.\n\nArgs:\n    name: Optional name for the tool (defaults to function name)\n    title: Optional human-readable title for the tool\n    description: Optional description of what the tool does\n    annotations: Optional ToolAnnotations providing additional tool information\n    icons: Optional list of icons for the tool\n    meta: Optional metadata dictionary for the tool\n    structured_output: Controls whether the tool's output is structured or unstructured\n        - If None, auto-detects based on the function's return type annotation\n        - If True, creates a structured tool (return type annotation permitting)\n        - If False, unconditionally creates an unstructured tool\n\nExample:\n    ```python\n    @server.tool()\n    def my_tool(x: int) -> str:\n        return str(x)\n    ```\n\n    ```python\n    @server.tool()\n    async def tool_with_context(x: int, ctx: Context) -> str:\n        await ctx.info(f\"Processing {x}\")\n        return str(x)\n    ```\n\n    ```python\n    @server.tool()\n    async def async_tool(x: int, context: Context) -> str:\n        await context.report_progress(50, 100)\n        return str(x)\n    ```\n\n--- call_tool ---\n(self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\nCall a tool by name with arguments.\n\nRaises:\n    ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n        tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n    UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n        its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n        exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n    MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n\n--- run ---\n(self, transport: \"Literal['stdio', 'sse', 'streamable-http']\" = 'stdio', **kwargs: 'Any') -> 'None'\n","metadata":{"output":"--- tool decorator ---\n(self, name: 'str | None' = None, title: 'str | None' = None, description: 'str | None' = None, annotations: 'ToolAnnotations | None' = None, icons: 'list[Icon] | None' = None, meta: 'dict[str, Any] | None' = None, structured_output: 'bool | None' = None) -> 'Callable[[_CallableT], _CallableT]'\nDecorator to register a tool.\n\nTools can optionally request a Context object by adding a parameter with the\nContext type annotation. The context provides access to MCP capabilities like\nlogging, progress reporting, and resource access.\n\nArgs:\n    name: Optional name for the tool (defaults to function name)\n    title: Optional human-readable title for the tool\n    description: Optional description of what the tool does\n    annotations: Optional ToolAnnotations providing additional tool information\n    icons: Optional list of icons for the tool\n    meta: Optional metadata dictionary for the tool\n    structured_output: Controls whether the tool's output is structured or unstructured\n        - If None, auto-detects based on the function's return type annotation\n        - If True, creates a structured tool (return type annotation permitting)\n        - If False, unconditionally creates an unstructured tool\n\nExample:\n    ```python\n    @server.tool()\n    def my_tool(x: int) -> str:\n        return str(x)\n    ```\n\n    ```python\n    @server.tool()\n    async def tool_with_context(x: int, ctx: Context) -> str:\n        await ctx.info(f\"Processing {x}\")\n        return str(x)\n    ```\n\n    ```python\n    @server.tool()\n    async def async_tool(x: int, context: Context) -> str:\n        await context.report_progress(50, 100)\n        return str(x)\n    ```\n\n--- call_tool ---\n(self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\nCall a tool by name with arguments.\n\nRaises:\n    ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n        tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n    UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n        its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n        exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n    MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n\n--- run ---\n(self, transport: \"Literal['stdio', 'sse', 'streamable-http']\" = 'stdio', **kwargs: 'Any') -> 'None'\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"source venv/bin/activate && python << 'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint('--- tool decorator ---')\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.tool))\nprint(inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.tool)[:2000])\nprint()\nprint('--- call_tool ---')\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint(inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.call_tool)[:800])\nprint()\nprint('--- run ---')\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.run))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788280634807,"end":1788280634850}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The early-year matches are only: 2021-01 (139), 2021-02 (52), 2016-01 (1) — all COVID-era tail of previous season (2016-01 is tail of 2015 season, 2021-01/02 tail of 2020 season). April matches (2018-04, 2019-04, 2022-04, 2023-04) are legit season openers of that year. December matches are season ends of that calendar year. So the rule: **for BR-Football league matches, if month is January or February, season = year - 1; else season = year.** That fixes 2020/2021 double-counting. \n\nLet me verify: after this rule, Serie A 2021 should have ~380 and 2020 ~264+139+52=455? Hmm, 2020 season = matches dated 2020 (264) + Jan 2021 (139) + Feb 2021 (52) = 455. Still >380. Hmm, that's too many. Wait, but the Jan 2021 matches include Serie A, B, C mixed. Let me compute per-tournament. Serie A 2020 had 304 matches played? No wait, 2020 Série A had all 380 matches (ended Feb 2021). BR-Football '2020' Serie A = 264, and the tail in Jan-Feb 2021. 264 + tail... Let me compute exactly.\n\nAlso note 2016-01: 1 match — tail of 2015 season. My rule handles it.\n\nBut careful — Copa do Brasil matches in Jan/Feb? Copa do Brasil runs Feb/March to November. BR-Football Copa do Brasil: e.g. first rounds in February — those belong to that year's cup. So the Jan/Feb->prev-year rule applies ONLY to Serie A/B/C, NOT Copa do Brasil. Good: apply rule to league tournaments only.\n\nLet me verify counts after the rule. Then design the dedup:\n\n**Dedup design (final):**\n- All matches loaded with canonical teams, canonical competition, season (explicit or derived), date, score, source dataset id.\n- Unified list `all_matches`.\n- For analytics that must not double-count (standings, team records, h2h, win rates), use a \"preferred\" subset per (competition, season): rank sources: for Série A: ['brasileirao_matches' (2012+), 'novo_campeonato' (2003-2019), 'br_football']; for Série B/C: ['br_football']; Copa do Brasil: ['brazilian_cup' (2012-2021), 'br_football' (2022+)]; Libertadores: ['libertadores'].\n- Actually simpler: order sources by priority per competition and keep a match only if its (competition, season, home, away, date) not already covered... but date mismatches (3/24 disagree). Hmm.\n\nEven simpler and robust: for each (competition, season), pick ONE authoritative source dataset (the best one), and compute all dedup-sensitive analytics from that source only. The other datasets' matches remain searchable individually (source filter), and extended stats from BR-Football attach by (competition, season, home, away) matching.\n\nPriority per competition-season:\n- Brasileirão Série A: novo (2003-2019), brasileirao_matches (2012-2022), br_football (2023). For 2012-2019 both novo & brasileirao exist — identical data? Both have 380/season. Prefer... brasileirao_matches (cleaner round info? novo has Arena/Vencedor). Hmm — novo has stadium info; equal really. I'll prefer 'brasileirao_matches' for 2012-2022 since it's the dedicated file with explicit state suffixes and rounds 1-38; novo for 2003-2011.\n- Série B, Série C: br_football only.\n- Copa do Brasil: brazilian_cup (2012-2021), br_football (2022-2023).\n- Libertadores: libertadores only.\n\nThis \"authoritative source per (competition, season)\" model is clean, avoids all double-counting, and every query tool can filter by competition+season and use authoritative matches by default (with an option to include all sources).\n\nFor h2h across competitions: sum over competitions, each using its authoritative source per season. \n\n**Match search** (\"Show me all Flamengo vs Fluminense matches\"): default should probably use authoritative matches to avoid duplicates in results. I'll add `include_all_sources=False` default. Hmm, but then \"What competitions has Palmeiras played in?\" — searching all sources finds Série A/B/C/Copa/Libertadores. With authoritative-per-(comp,season) there are no duplicates anyway. Actually the authoritative model solves both: the unified \"primary view\" is a complete deduplicated corpus. Non-authoritative matches (e.g. BR-Football 2015 Serie A with corner stats) are still in `all_matches` for stats attachment and source-specific search.\n\nLet me now define the **authoritative (primary) match set** precisely:\n- For (comp, season) pairs: \n  - Série A: novo if 2003 <= season <= 2011; brasileirao_matches if 2012 <= season <= 2022; br_football if season == 2023 (and 2012-2013 if not covered? brasileirao covers 2012+; fine).\n  - Série B/C: br_football (all seasons).\n  - Copa do Brasil: brazilian_cup if 2012 <= season <= 2021; br_football if season >= 2022.\n  - Libertadores: libertadores.\n- What about Série A 2014 in br_football (281 matches, partial)? Covered by brasileirao_matches anyway.\n\nDetermination can be data-driven: build a source priority list per competition and for each (comp, season) choose the first source having >= some threshold of matches? Data-driven is fragile; hardcode priorities but VERIFY with counts (e.g., Série A seasons should have 380 matches, 20 teams; assert in tests for known seasons).\n\n**Standings**: Série A 2003-2004 had 552 matches (24 teams), 2005 462 (22 teams), 2006+ 380 (20 teams). Standings computed from primary Série A matches per season. Validate champions: 2003 Cruzeiro, ..., 2022 Palmeiras. For BR-Football Série B/C 2023 — standings fine.\n\nEdge: Série A 2023 from BR-Football: 377 matches (380 minus a few missing) — standings approximate. Note in output that it's calculated from available matches.\n\n**Relegation**: bottom 4 in Série A (since 2006; 2003-2005 different). I'll just report bottom 4 (and note count varies by era — keep simple: bottom 4, since most seasons in data have 20 teams).\n\n**Now the MCP server (mcp 2.x)**: \n- `from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer`, `server = MCPServer(\"brazilian-soccer\")`, decorate tools with `@server.tool()`. Let me check the decorator signature and call_tool API. Also check how to run: `server.run()` (stdio). Let me introspect.\n\nLet me check `MCPServer.tool` and `MCPServer.call_tool` signatures.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dd4f973001dmOiH9rid4f4Gx","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05dd4a6dd001WAVoz0S3x8TcCf"}}
