ver.tool()` decorator, `server.call_tool(name, args)` (sync method, returns CallToolResult), `server.run()` for stdio.\n- `list_tools()` presumably returns tools.\n\nNow I have everything needed. Let me design the full implementation:\n\n## Project structure (all in current dir)\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py          # version, exports\n  models.py            # Match, Player dataclasses\n  normalize.py         # team/competition/date normalization + alias tables\n  loader.py            # CSV -> normalized Match/Player objects, SoccerData repository\n  service.py           # query/analysis API (the \"brain\")\n  server.py            # MCP server: tool definitions wrapping service\n  __main__.py          # entry: python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp\ntests/\n  conftest.py          # session-scoped SoccerData fixture\n  test_normalize_bdd.py        # BDD: name variations\n  test_match_queries_bdd.py    # BDD: match search, h2h, last meeting\n  test_team_queries_bdd.py     # BDD: team records\n  test_player_queries_bdd.py   # BDD: player search\n  test_competition_queries_bdd.py  # BDD: standings, champions, relegation, seasons\n  test_statistics_bdd.py       # BDD: avg goals, home advantage, biggest wins, best records\n  test_server_bdd.py           # BDD: MCP server tools list + call\nrequirements.txt\nREADME-updated? -> update README.md at end (TASK.md doesn't require README update; the sample prompt mentions it but TASK.md is authoritative: no README requirement. I'll still add brief usage instructions to README — helpful and harmless.)\n```\n\nEach code file starts with a detailed context block comment (per the sample prompt's \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — that's from prompts.txt sample prompt, not TASK.md. TASK.md doesn't require it, but it's good practice and matches the guide's spirit. I'll include concise module docstrings — the instructions say don't add comments unless asked... The system prompt says \"IMPORTANT: DO NOT ADD ***ANY*** COMMENTS unless asked\". Module docstrings are documentation, and the task context (prompts.txt shows the benchmark's typical prompt asks for detailed context block comments). Hmm — TASK.md is what I'm asked to implement. It doesn't ask for comments. I'll use docstrings (not comments) since docstrings are API documentation and pytest BDD structure benefits from Given/When/Then docstrings. GWT-structured tests inherently need the Given/When/Then structure visible — I'll express that via docstrings and function organization, not comments.\n\n## Detailed design\n\n### models.py\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None\n    time: str | None\n    competition: str          # canonical competition display name\n    season: int | None\n    round: str | None          # display: \"Round 22\" | \"Final\" | \"Group Stage\" | None\n    home_team: str             # canonical display name\n    away_team: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    venue: str | None           # stadium\n    stage: str | None\n    source: str                # dataset id\n    # extended stats (may be None)\n    home_corners / away_corners / home_shots / away_shots / home_attacks / away_attacks: int | None\n    def result_relative_to(team) -> 'W'/'D'/'L' | None\n    def winner / loser ...\n    @property margin\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Player:\n    id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, preferred_foot, value, wage, height, weight  (+ skills dict)\n```\n\n### normalize.py\n- `strip_accents(s)`\n- UF set + country codes for Libertadores: map (URU)->uru tag etc.\n- `normalize_team_name(raw) -> (canonical_id, display_name)`\n  Steps:\n  1. strip, NFKD accents, casefold\n  2. replace non-alphanumeric with space, collapse\n  3. ALIAS lookup (dict normalized-alias -> canonical_id). Aliases include FIFA full names, bare famous names, spelled-out forms.\n  4. If not found: detect trailing 2-letter UF token (or known country code) -> canonical_id = base-uf (e.g. \"america-mg\"); else base alone.\n  5. ALIAS lookup again on base (without state) for defaults: e.g. \"flamengo\" -> \"flamengo-rj\".\n  Wait — order matters: \"flamengo-rj\" shouldn't alias-strip to \"flamengo\" -> then default back \"flamengo-rj\" (idempotent, fine). But \"bahia de feira\" must NOT become \"bahia-ba\". So defaults only for exact famous base names, listed explicitly.\n  \n  Hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: do I even want canonical ids WITH state suffixes like \"flamengo-rj\"? What matters:\n  - \"Flamengo-RJ\" (Brasileirao), \"Flamengo\" (novo, br, libertadores), FIFA \"Flamengo\"? (not in FIFA), all -> same id \"flamengo-rj\"? Hmm, but hmm, wait: what does 'Vasco da Gama-RJ' -> \"vasca da gama-rj\"?? normalize \"vasco da gama-rj\" -> tokens [vasco, da, gama, rj] -> \"vasco da gama-rj\". And BR-Football 'Vasco Da Gama RJ' -> same. And Libertadores 'Vasco' -> alias \"vasco\" -> \"vasco da gama-rj\"? I'd alias 'vasco' -> 'vasco da gama-rj'... but display \"Vasco da Gama\". Hmm, actually simpler canonical id: \"vasco\". Strip 'da gama' via alias: 'vasco da gama' -> 'vasco'. Yes! Canonical id \"vasco\", display \"Vasco da Gama\"... but wait — display for novo file is \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\". Display name: \"Vasco da Gama\". \n\nLet me simplify: canonical ids = short common names (lowercase, unaccented): flamengo, fluminense, vasco, botafogo-rj... \n\nAmbiguity requires suffix only for: botafogo (rj/pb/sp), america (mg/rn/sp), atletico/goianiense... hmm wait \"atletico\" alone is never a full name; spelled-out forms: atletico mineiro -> atletico-mg; atletico paranaense -> atletico-pr; atletico goianiense -> atletico-go; atletico acreano -> atletico-ac(reano?); Atlético Acreano is Rio Branco-AC club... it's \"atletico acreano\" — keep as is (distinct enough).\n- vitoria (ba/es): 'vitoria' bare = vitoria-ba (default); 'vitoria-es' distinct; 'vitoria da conquista' distinct; 'vitoria ec'/'ec vitoria' -> vitoria-ba.\n- santos (sp/ap): 'santos' bare = santos-sp; 'santos-ap' distinct. BUT Libertadores 'Santos' = santos-sp. 'Santos Laguna' (Mexican) — distinct via alias 'santos laguna' -> itself (two tokens, no clash).\n- gremio (rs / novo? 'gremio novorizontino', 'gremio anapolis' distinct multiword). 'gremio' bare -> gremio-rs.\n- csa (al): 'csa' -> csa-al.\n- national (uru/par): Libertadores 'Nacional (URU)'/'Nacional-URU' -> 'nacional-uru'; 'Nacional (PAR)'/'Nacional-PAR' -> 'nacional-par'. Bare 'Nacional' in Brasileirao? 'Nacional-AM'/'Nacional-SP'/'Nacional-PR'? If appear with suffix fine.\n\nSo canonical id scheme: lowercase base + '-uf' suffix ONLY for ambiguous clubs (those having multiple state variants in data) + '-country' for foreign clubs in Libertadores.\n\nPractical approach: I'll define the alias table with ~60 curated entries for major/ambiguous clubs, each canonical id -> display name. The normalizer:\n1. normalized string n = clean(raw)\n2. if n in ALIAS: return ALIAS[n]\n3. strip trailing uf/country tag if present -> (base, tag)\n4. if base in ALIAS and (tag is None or ALIAS[base] ends with... hmm)\n\nWait, simpler: do (3) first, then look up base in DEFAULT_SUFFIX (base -> canonical for famous clubs where bare form maps to a specific one). Order:\n1. n = clean(raw)\n2. if n in ALIAS (full-string aliases incl. FIFA names): canonical = ALIAS[n]\n3. else: split trailing tag if valid UF or country code; if tag: canonical_id = f\"{base}-{tag}\"; else canonical_id = base\n4. if canonical_id in DEFAULTS: canonical_id = DEFAULTS[canonical_id]  (e.g. bare 'flamengo' -> 'flamengo-rj'?? or should flamengo have no suffix?)\n\nHmm, I keep flip-flopping on suffix style. DECIDE: \n\nCanonical ids WITH state for clubs whose name needs it. Which need it? botafogo, america, atletico (mg/go/pr as atletico-mg etc.), vitoria, santos(ap vs sp — but 'santos-ap' is a tiny club appearing only in lower divisions; keep suffix), gremio (gremio-rs vs gremio novorizontino — the latter is multiword, distinct by name; so bare 'gremio' can just be 'gremio'!). \n\nUgh. OK here's the final clean rule — I'll enumerate canonical ids explicitly in a CLUBS table for every club that any major query would touch, with aliases. For everything else, canonical id = normalized string (with trailing state suffix preserved as '-uf' if present, country tag '-xxx' for foreign). Bare famous bases that appear in multiple files get entries in the table (flamengo, palmeiras, corinthians, sao paulo, santos, vasco, gremio, internacional, cruzeiro, fluminense, botafogo, bahia, sport, vitoria, ceara, fortaleza, goias, coritiba, athletico-pr, atletico-mg, atletico-go, chapecoense, criciuma, avai, figueirense, juventude, nautico, csa, cuiaba, parana, bragantino, etc.) mapping bare -> canonical WITH suffix for those ambiguous, or WITHOUT for unique ones. The table keys are canonical ids; consistency verified by a test that counts unique teams per Série A season (should be exactly 20 for 2012-2022, 24 for 2003-2004, 22 for 2005).\n\nThat's a strong test! If Flamengo split into 'flamengo' and 'flamengo-rj', the 2019 Série A would show 21 teams. \n\nLet me define canonical ids WITHOUT suffix when unambiguous, WITH suffix when ambiguous:\n- flamengo, fluminense, corinthians, palmeiras, sao-paulo, santos (sp), vasco, cruzeiro, gremio, internacional, bahia, sport, ceara, fortaleza, goias, coritiba, chapecoense, criciuma, avai, figueirense, juventude, nautico, cuiaba, parana, bragantino, athletico-pr, atletico-mg, atletico-go, botafogo-rj, america-mg, america-rn, vitoria-ba, vitoria-es, atletico-goianiense?? no—goianiense=atletico-go. csa (al? 'csa' bare is unambiguous — CSA = Clube Social e... hmm there's also CSA-AL only famous one; keep 'csa')...\n\nHmm wait, but there's a catch: if canonical is 'flamengo' (no suffix) then trailing-suffix stripping must remove 'rj' from 'flamengo-rj'. Rule 3 handles that (strip trailing UF). But then 'botafogo-pb' -> 'botafogo-pb' (suffix KEPT because... rule strips it!). PROBLEM: rule 3 strips ALL trailing UFs. 'botafogo-pb' would become 'botafogo' -> collide with 'botafogo-rj'.\n\nResolution: rule 3 strips trailing UF ONLY IF the base is in the BARE_OK set (clubs whose canonical has no suffix)? Or reverse: strip only if (base, uf) maps to known canonical? Cleanest: after computing base+tag, look up ALIAS[base+'-'+tag] first (suffixed alias), then ALIAS[base] (bare alias). The ALIAS dict maps EVERY normalized variant explicitly to canonical ids, including:\n- 'flamengo-rj' -> flamengo, 'flamengo' -> flamengo, 'fluminense-rj'-> fluminense, 'botafogo-rj'-> botafogo-rj, 'botafogo'-> botafogo-rj, 'botafogo-pb'-> botafogo-pb, 'botafogo-sp'->botafogo-sp...\n- 'america-mg' -> america-mg, 'america rn'/'america fc natal'-> america-rn, 'america' -> america-mg?? hmm bare 'america' — where does it appear bare? In novo 2003-2019? Sample showed 'América-MG','América-RN' with suffixes. Bare 'América' might appear in Libertadores ('América de Cali' is Colombian — separate). I'll map bare 'america' -> america-mg if needed. Eh — skip; if it appears bare somewhere unmapped it stays 'america' as its own club. Fine as long as no split: does any club appear as BOTH 'america' and 'america-mg' in the same competition? Copa do Brasil uses 'America - MG' (suffix). BR-Football 'America MG' (suffix). novo/brasileirao suffixed. FIFA 'América FC (Minas Gerais)' -> alias. OK.\n\nRather than agonize, let me AUTO-GENERATE the variant->canonical mapping from a curated CANONICAL table:\n\n```\nCLUBS = {\n  'flamengo': ('Flamengo', ['flamengo', 'flamengo rj', 'flamengo-rj', 'crf flamengo', ...]),\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nI'll write the table with canonical id, display name, and alias list (normalized). The normalizer:\n1. clean(raw)\n2. exact match in alias index -> canonical\n3. else strip trailing UF/country -> (base, tag); exact match of base in alias index -> if canonical for base exists use it... no wait, that would map 'botafogo pb' base 'botafogo' -> botafogo-rj. WRONG.\n\nSo: no automatic bare-base fallback. Only EXPLICIT aliases. Then variants not in table keep their computed id. The risk: a variant I didn't enumerate splits a club across files. Mitigate via the \"20 teams per season\" verification + standings champion checks. Let me enumerate systematically: I'll write a script that, using a first-pass canonicalizer, lists all distinct ids per Série A season and flags where a season has >expected teams or where a known club is missing. Then I patch the alias table until all seasons are consistent. Also check h2h counts (e.g. Fla-Flu in Série A should be ~2/season 2012-2022 + 2003-2011... novo 2003-2011 = 9 seasons × 2 + 2012-2022 11 seasons × 2 = 40 total Série A meetings; spec example says \"15 more matches in dataset\" — whatever, dataset-dependent).\n\nFor the country tags (Libertadores): '(URU)', '-URU', '-EQU', '(PAR)', '-PAR', '(PER)', '(VEN)', '(COL)', '(ARG)', '(BOL)', '(CHI)', '(ECU)', '-EQU'... Let me normalize those trailing tags to lowercase country code. 'Barcelona-EQU' -> 'barcelona-equa'? No: keep as 'barcelona-equ'? Hmm — but 'Emelec' (Ecuador) is bare; 'Delfín' and 'Delfín-EQU' BOTH exist (same club, Ecuadorean!). So 'delfin-equ' and 'delfin' split. ALIAS 'delfin' -> 'delfin-equ'... or normalize '-EQU' to nothing? Ecuadorean clubs: Barcelona, Emelec, Delfín, Independiente Del Valle, Independiente Petrolero... I'll add explicit aliases: 'delfin equ' -> 'delfin'; 'independiente del valle' variants; 'guarani par'->'guarani'; 'libertad par'->'libertad'; 'olimpia par'->'olimpia'; 'nacional uru'->'nacional-uru'; 'nacional par'->'nacional-par'; 'river plate uru'->'river plate uru' hmm River Plate (ARG) vs River Plate (URU) both in data! 'river plate' bare (ARG), 'river plate-uru'/'river plate uru' (URU). Keep distinct ids: 'river plate' (ARG), 'river plate-uru'.\n\nOK the plan is solid. Foreign clubs get ids like 'nacional-uru', 'barcelona-equ' (EQU→equ? or 'ecu'? keep raw tag lowercase: 'barcelona-equ', 'bolivia'→'bolivar'). 'Barcelona-EQU': EQU = Ecuador. Keep 'barcelona-equ'.\n\n### Competition normalization\n- 'Brasileirao_Matches.csv' -> 'Brasileirão Série A'\n- 'novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv' -> 'Brasileirão Série A'\n- 'Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv' -> 'Copa do Brasil'\n- 'Libertadores_Matches.csv' -> 'Copa Libertadores'\n- BR-Football tournament: 'Serie A' -> 'Brasileirão Série A'; 'Serie B' -> 'Brasileirão Série B'; 'Serie C' -> 'Brasileirão Série C'; 'Copa do Brasil' -> 'Copa do Brasil'\n\nCompetition matching for user queries: accept aliases ('brasileirao', 'serie a', 'série a', 'copa do brasil', 'libertadores', 'serie b', 'serie c', 'brasileirão').\n\n### Date parsing\n- 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' / 'YYYY-MM-DD'\n- 'DD/MM/YYYY'\n- 'NA' -> None\n\n### loader.py\n- `SoccerData` class: holds matches (list[Match]), players (list[Player]), primary_matches (deduplicated authoritative view), indexes: by team, by (competition, season), team name index (display + id + aliases), player name index.\n- ` SoccerData.load(data_dir=...)` classmethod.\n- Extended stats: BR-Football rows attach to primary matches? For simplicity, extended stats live on BR-Football Match objects themselves (searchable via source). Also build `stats_lookup`: (comp, season, home_id, away_id) -> stats row for corner stats attached onto primary matches when found (only for display richness). Hmm — attaching BR-Football stats to primary matches by fixture key (comp, season, home, away) — the Série A 2023 BR-Football matches ARE primary. For 2014-2022 Série A, BR-Football matches are non-primary but hold stats. When user searches matches (primary view), stats won't show unless attached. I'll attach: for each non-primary BR-Football league/cup match, if (comp, season, home, away) exists in primary index, copy its corner/shot/attack stats onto the primary Match (create new frozen dataclass instance via replace). This makes cross-file integration real (\"Cross-file queries work\"). Since primary per (comp, season, home, away) is unique for leagues (each pair meets once at each venue per season) — for cups, pairs can meet twice... BR-Football Copa do Brasil also has fixtures; attaching by pair may mismatch legs. Only attach when date also matches OR pair unique. I'll key stats by (comp, season, home, away, date) first, fallback (comp, season, home, away) if unique. Eh — simpler: attach only exact (comp, season, home, away, date) matches... but 3/24 dates disagree. OK: attach by (comp, season, home, away) when that key is unique in primary (league play: yes; cup: check uniqueness). Good enough.\n\n### service.py — `SoccerQueryService`\nMethods (each returns dict/list ready for JSON):\n- `find_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, venue=None('home'|'away'|'any'), stage=None, limit=50, offset=0)` -> {'matches': [...], 'total': n}\n- `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, limit=None)` -> matches + summary {a_wins, b_wins, draws, goals}\n- `last_meeting(team_a, team_b)` -> match or None\n- `team_record(team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all')` -> {matches, wins, draws, losses, gf, ga, win_rate, competitions breakdown}\n- `team_profile(team)` -> {competitions: [...], seasons per competition, overall record, first/last match}\n- `standings(competition, season)` -> table sorted by pts, gd, gf; flags champion, relegated (bottom 4), promoted? (top 4 for Série B/C)\n- `competition_info(competition=None, season=None)` -> seasons available, match counts\n- `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall, max_overall, limit)` -> players sorted by overall\n- `top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10)`\n- `club_squad(club)` -> players at club (canonical team match, e.g. 'Flamengo' finds FIFA players at Flamengo — none in this dataset! But 'Grêmio', 'Santos' exist. Fine.)\n- `brazilian_players_by_club()` -> Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs grouped\n- `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` -> from DERBY table (name + pair of canonical ids), find matches\n- `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` -> sorted by margin\n- `average_goals(competition=None, season=None)` -> {avg_goals_per_match, home_win_rate, draw_rate, away_win_rate, total_matches}\n- `best_home_records(competition, season)` / `best_away_records` — spec asks \"Which team has the best away record?\" -> compute across competition-season (or all seasons? aggregate). I'll support competition+season filters and default to all-time aggregate within competition... For 'best record' queries, all-time across primary matches.\n\nDERBY table (canonical ids):\n- Fla-Flu: flamengo–fluminense\n- Clássico Vovô: flamengo–vasco\n- Derby Paulista: palmeiras–corinthians\n- Majestoso: sao-paulo–corinthians\n- Choque-Rei: palmeiras–sao-paulo\n- Grenal: gremio–internacional\n- Clássico Mineiro: atletico-mg–cruzeiro (cruzeiro canonical no suffix — unambiguous? Cruzeiro-EC? 'Cruzeiro' bare everywhere + FIFA 'Cruzeiro' -> 'cruzeiro'. fine)\n- Ba-Vi: bahia–vitoria-ba\n- Clássico dos Gigantes? vasco–fluminense? hmm skip\n- Clássico Alvinegro? coritiba... skip\n- Clássico Re-Pa: remo–paysandu (probably not in data — Série B/C? Remo/Paysandu appear in Serie C maybe)\n- Nordeste: sport–csa? Bahia–Sport? skip minor\nKeep ~8 solid ones.\n\n### server.py — MCP tools\n- `search_matches`, `head_to_head`, `last_meeting`, `team_record`, `team_profile`, `standings`, `competition_info` (list competitions + seasons), `search_players`, `top_players`, `club_squad`, `brazilian_players_by_club`, `derbies`, `biggest_wins`, `stats_summary` (avg goals/home rates), `best_records` (best home/away), `find_team` (resolve user string to canonical club + variants), maybe `sample_questions`? The success criteria: \"At least 20 sample questions can be answered\" — I'll add a `list_questions`? No — that's meta. The 16 tools cover far more than 20 question types. I'll document 20+ sample questions in README with the tools that answer them.\n\nTool count ~16. Each returns JSON-serializable structures (dicts/lists). FastMCP serializes return values; with mcp 2.x MCPServer, returning dict gives structured content. To be safe and simple, return strings? No — dicts are fine and richer; spec's \"answer format\" examples show formatted text blocks though! E.g. h2h returns a formatted text answer. Hmm: \"Returns properly formatted responses\". I'll return dicts that include a 'summary' formatted string plus structured 'matches' list. Best of both.\n\nActually — simpler and very robust: each tool returns a dict with structured data; several also include a human-readable 'summary' string following the spec's example formats. \n\n### Tests (BDD GWT)\nUse pytest. Structure each test with Given/When/Then in docstring (Gherkin style per spec) + comments marking sections? \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\" — TASK.md asks for BDD GWT structured tests. GWT structure can be expressed via docstrings and blank-line separation; I'll use section headers as plain comments? The instruction says no comments unless asked. The task asks for BDD GWT structure — Given/When/Then labels are intrinsic to that. I'll use Gherkin docstrings + named helper locals (given_, when_, then_)? Overkill. I'll write docstrings with Gherkin scenarios (that's documentation, standard for BDD) and structure test bodies in three phases. Minimal plain comments like `# Given` are standard pytest-bdd practice... but to respect the no-comments rule I'll rely on docstrings for the GWT labels and phase separation via blank lines. Hmm, actually honestly `# Given / # When / # Then` comments make GWT explicit and are what \"GWT structured PyTest\" means. The user's TASK.md explicitly asks for BDD/GWT structure, which I interpret as permission (being \"asked\"). I'll use Gherkin docstrings AND keep bodies clean without inline comments. Docstrings carry the Gherkin.\n\nTest coverage plan (validated against real data):\n1. **Loading**: all 6 files load; counts > 0; total primary matches count; performance < 5s.\n2. **Normalization**: \"Palmeiras-SP\", \"Palmeiras\", \"Palmeiras - SP\" -> same; \"Athletico-PR\" == \"Atlético-PR\" == \"Athletico Paranaense\" == \"Atletico-PR\"; \"São Paulo\" == \"Sao Paulo\" == \"sao paulo fc\"; date parsing (ISO, Brazilian, datetime).\n3. **Match queries**: \n   - Flamengo vs Fluminense matches exist, each has date/score/competition; Série A count per season == 2.\n   - Palmeiras 2023 matches (Série A 2023 from BR-Football) > 0.\n   - Copa do Brasil finals: Libertadores... no, Copa do Brasil 'final' round? Round column is 1-8. Hmm \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — round 8? Hmm round values 1-8: Copa do Brasil rounds: 1st round... final = round 8? Wait actually let me check: 1337 matches, round 1-8. Round 7 = semifinal? 8 = final? Let me check round 8 count — should be 2 legs × 10 seasons = 20ish.\n   - Date range queries.\n   - last_meeting(Flamengo, Corinthians) returns most recent.\n4. **Team records**: Palmeiras 2023 season record; Corinthians home record 2022 (spec example!); most goals in Série A 2023.\n5. **Players**: search \"Gabriel Barbosa\" (spec example — is he in FIFA data? FIFA 19 has Gabriel Barbosa at... let me check!); Brazilian players count 827; top Brazilian players (Neymar? is Neymar in data — FIFA 19 yes, 'Neymar Jr' at PSG overall 92); players at Grêmio (20); positions filter.\n6. **Competitions**: standings 2019 -> champion Flamengo; 2022 -> Palmeiras; 2003 -> Cruzeiro; relegated 2020 (Coritiba, Bahia, Botafogo-RJ? 2020 relegated: Coritiba, Bahia, Botafogo, Vasco — hmm 2020 Série A relegated were Coritiba, Bahia, Botafogo-RJ, Vasco da Gama. I think. Let me verify from data and use whatever data says — validate champion only from external knowledge which I'm confident about: 2019 Flamengo (90 pts, 28W 6D 4L — from spec example!), 2022 Palmeiras (81 pts), 2021 Atlético-MG (84?), 2003 Cruzeiro, 2011 Corinthians (71?). I'll assert champions for a handful of seasons I'm sure about.)\n   - Copa Libertadores 2019 champion Flamengo (final vs River Plate 2-1)... the Libertadores file has finals 2013-2022. 2019 final: Flamengo 2-1 River Plate (single match in Santiago). In data? season 2019, stage 'final'. Let me check.\n7. **Statistics**: avg goals per match Série A ~2.4-2.6; home win rate ~0.45-0.55; biggest wins include Santos 8-0 Bolívar? (spec example mentions Santos 8-0 Bolivar 2012-05-27 Libertadores — wait, 2012 Libertadores isn't in data (2013+). Let me check biggest wins in Libertadores data. Also biggest Série A wins.)\n8. **Server**: tools list contains all expected tool names; call_tool('search_matches', {...}) returns results; call head_to_head via server.call_tool; unknown tool raises.\n\nLet me check a few data facts before writing tests:\n- Gabriel Barbosa in fifa_data?\n- Neymar Jr in fifa_data?\n- Copa do Brasil round 8 = final? round distribution.\n- Libertadores 2019 final row.\n- Biggest wins by margin in each dataset.\n\nThen write code. Let me also think about the \"20 sample questions\" — I'll put a table in README mapping 20+ questions to tools.\n\nPerformance: loading 42k rows with csv module: fast. Build indexes. All queries < 1s. Tests: module-scoped fixture loads once.\n\nLet me check those data facts now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dd5bdfd001Bir3dAcfLDEMjj","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05dd4ff5b001ntyLxbUfPGpJM5"}}
