a Nova-GO\" (hypothetical): \"vila nova go\"; not in aliases; tag=go; base=\"vila nova\" not in ALIAS, not AMBIGUOUS → return \"vila nova-go\". Hmm — but wait: is that right? Vila Nova-GO is the only \"vila nova\". The suffix stays. That's fine (consistent as long as all files spell it the same way — 'Vila Nova-GO'... BR-Football might have 'Vila Nova' bare? If so → \"vila nova\" ≠ \"vila nova-go\" — split! Let me check... Actually for safety, I should strip suffix when base is NOT ambiguous and NOT in aliases: \"vila nova go\" → base \"vila nova\" unambiguous → strip tag → \"vila nova\"? But then \"Criciuma-SC\" → \"criciuma\" ✓ consistent with bare \"Criciuma\". Hmm wait but then what about clubs where the state IS part of distinguishing like... \"America-MG\" — base \"america\" IS ambiguous → keep suffix ✓. \"Botafogo-PB\" — ambiguous → keep ✓.\n\nActually YES — the rule should be: **if base is not ambiguous, strip the tag** (return base, or alias lookup of base). That makes \"Vila Nova-GO\" and \"Vila Nova\" both → \"vila nova\". And \"Cuiaba-MT\"/\"Cuiaba\" → \"cuiaba\" (cuiaba in aliases anyway). But wait — what about foreign clubs: \"Nacional (URU)\" → tokens [nacional, uru], tag=uru, base=\"nacional\" ∈ AMBIGUOUS → \"nacional-uru\" ✓. \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"barcelona\" not in AMBIGUOUS... → would strip tag → \"barcelona\"?? But then collides with... only Barcelona-EQU exists in Libertadores. But keep the equ tag for display \"Barcelona (EQU)\". Hmm — \"barcelona\" not ambiguous (no other barcelona) → strip → \"barcelona\". Display would be \"Barcelona\". Acceptable... but wait, actually there might be \"Barcelona SC\" (Ecuador) vs FC Barcelona? Not in data. It's fine.\n\nBut careful with \"Guaraní-PAR\" → base \"guarani\" ∈ AMBIGUOUS → keep \"guarani-par\" ✓. \"Libertad-PAR\" → \"libertad par\" in aliases → \"libertad\" ✓. \n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtle problem with stripping tags for unambiguous bases: the FIFA club \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → cleaned \"america fc minas gerais\" → in aliases → \"america-mg\" ✓. \"Paraná\" (FIFA) → \"parana\" → aliases → \"parana\" ✓. \n\nWhat about \"Atlético Nacional\" (Colombian, Libertadores) → \"atletico nacional\" — not in aliases; tokens [atletico, nacional]; no tag (last token \"nacional\" is 8 letters, not tag); base = \"atletico nacional\"; not in ALIAS; not AMBIGUOUS (full base \"atletico nacional\" ≠ \"atletico\"); return \"atletico nacional\" ✓ distinct from atletico-mg. \n\n\"Atlético Tucumán\" → \"atletico tucuman\" ✓ own id.\n\nOK so I need to change the flow: when tag present and base NOT ambiguous → return alias(base) if base in aliases else base. Wait but hmm — what about \"Sport-PE\"? \"sport pe\" → in aliases → \"sport\" ✓ (alias hit before tag logic). \"Ceara-CE\" → \"ceara ce\"... is \"ceara ce\" in aliases? Yes I listed it. But if it weren't: tag=ce, base=\"ceara\" unambiguous → \"ceara\" ✓.\n\nHmm, but WAIT: there are cases where different clubs share the same base but I haven't marked them ambiguous! E.g. \"Santos-FC\"? What about \"Andrade\"? Let me think about actual collisions in the data:\n- 'Paysandu-PA'? vs bare 'Paysandu' — same club ✓ strip fine.\n- 'Remo-PA'? bare 'Remo' — fine.\n- 'CRB-AL' vs 'CRB'? BR-Football might have 'CRB' bare; Copa do Brasil 'CRB - AL'. If one file uses 'CRB-AL' → base 'crb' unambiguous → strip → 'crb' ✓ same as bare. \n- 'ABC-RN' / 'ABC - RN' / 'ABC' → 'abc' ✓ (RN stripped).\n- 'ASA-AL' / 'ASA AL' / 'A.s.a. - AL' → 'asa' ✓.\n- 'Confiança-SE'? → 'confianca' ✓.\n- Two DIFFERENT clubs sharing a base without state in data... e.g. 'Rio Branco' (multiple states), 'Nacional' (handled), 'Atlético' variants (all spelled out), 'America' (handled), 'Botafogo' (handled), 'Vitoria' (handled), 'Guarani' (handled), 'Santos' (sp only famous; santos-ap is multiword distinct? No — 'Santos-AP' → base 'santos' ambiguous → 'santos-ap' ✓ kept).\n- 'Internacional' vs 'Inter de Limeira'? 'Inter' (SC)? Hmm, 'Internacional-RS' vs 'Internacional' (SC? no, Internacional de Limeira is 'Inter de Limeira'). There's also 'Interporto'? Not an issue.\n- 'Gremio' vs 'Gremio Prudente' vs 'Gremio Novorizontino' — multiword distinct ✓. But wait 'Grêmio Prudente' — novo file! tokens [gremio, prudente] → own id ✓.\n\nOK. Now what about two UFs being actually part of the name? \"Palmeiras-SP\"... no cases like 'Santa Catarina club ending in UF code'... 'ABC-RN' fine.\n\nOne more important case: the Brasileirao file has 'Atletico-PR' AND novo has 'Athletico-PR' → both → 'athletico-pr' via aliases ✓. But what about FIFA 'Atlético Paranaense' → alias ✓ 'athletico-pr'.\n\nNow let me reconsider the flow order in code:\n\n```python\ndef normalize_team_id(raw):\n    cleaned = _clean(raw)\n    if cleaned in ALIAS: return ALIAS[cleaned]\n    tokens = cleaned.split()\n    base_tokens, tag = _split_tag(tokens)\n    base = \" \".join(base_tokens)\n    if base in AMBIGUOUS_BASES:\n        if tag: return f\"{base}-{tag}\"\n        return BARE_DEFAULTS.get(base, base)\n    if base in ALIAS:   # covers \"palmeiras sp\" -> palmeiras when full not in alias... wait\n        return ALIAS[base]\n    if tag is None and base_tokens != tokens: ... \n```\n\nHmm wait: if cleaned not in ALIAS but base (after tag strip) is in ALIAS — e.g. \"palmeiras sp\" IS in aliases already. \"fortaleza ce\" in aliases. But something like \"joinville sc\" IS in aliases. What about \"juventude rs\"? In aliases ✓. \"nautico pe\" ✓. Cases not in aliases: \"vila nova go\" → base \"vila nova\" not in ALIAS → strip → return base \"vila nova\" (unambiguous). \n\nBut hold on — what about when tag is None and the name ends with a club form token: \"EC Bahia\" → \"ec bahia\" IS in aliases → \"bahia\" ✓. \"Fortaleza FC\" → \"fortaleza fc\" in aliases ✓. Generic fallback strips form tokens anyway.\n\nAnd foreign: \"Boca Juniors\" → no tag → \"boca juniors\" ✓. \"Colon\" (Colón) → \"colon\" ✓. \"Newells Old Boys\" → strip trailing \"boys\"? No — 'boys' isn't a form token ✓ stays.\n\nHmm, one problem: CLUB_FORM_TOKENS stripping \"sc\" — \"SC Internacional\" → \"internacional\" ✓. But what about a club ending in a real word 'sc'?? None.\n\nWhat about \"Atletico Acreano\" — ends 'acreano' not form token ✓.\n\n\"Mineros de Guaiana\" ✓.\n\nNow the important flow bug in my current code: \n\n```python\nif base in ALIAS_TO_CANONICAL and (tag is None or base not in AMBIGUOUS_BASES):\n    return ALIAS_TO_CANONICAL[base]\n```\nFor \"vila nova go\": base=\"vila nova\" not in ALIAS → falls through; base not AMBIGUOUS; tag → return \"vila nova-go\". I want \"vila nova\" (strip). Change to: if tag and base not ambiguous → return alias(base) or base. Let me rewrite that function cleanly:\n\n```python\ndef normalize_team_id(raw: str) -> str:\n    cleaned = _clean(raw)\n    if not cleaned: return \"\"\n    if cleaned in ALIAS_TO_CANONICAL: return ALIAS_TO_CANONICAL[cleaned]\n    tokens = cleaned.split()\n    base_tokens, tag = _split_tag(tokens)\n    base = \" \".join(base_tokens)\n    if base in AMBIGUOUS_BASES:\n        if tag: return f\"{base}-{tag}\"\n        return BARE_DEFAULTS.get(base, base)\n    if base in ALIAS_TO_CANONICAL:\n        return ALIAS_TO_CANONICAL[base]\n    if tag and base:   # unambiguous base with state suffix: drop suffix\n        return base\n    # strip trailing club form tokens\n    trimmed = [t for t in base_tokens if t not in CLUB_FORM_TOKENS]\n    if trimmed and trimmed != base_tokens:\n        return normalize_team_id(\" \".join(trimmed))\n    return base\n```\n\nWait but there's a subtlety: \"atletico nacional\" — base = \"atletico nacional\" (no tag). Not ambiguous (≠ \"atletico\"). Return \"atletico nacional\" ✓.\n\nAnother subtlety: bare ambiguous WITHOUT default: \"nacional\" → BARE_DEFAULTS lacks 'nacional'... I put \"nacional\": \"nacional\". OK returns \"nacional\". Hmm — but is bare \"Nacional\" ever in data? Libertadores has tagged only. Copa do Brasil: \"Nacional - AM\"? or \"Nacional - SP\"? If 'Nacional - AM' → tag=am → \"nacional-am\" ✓ distinct.\n\n\"atletico\" bare → BARE_DEFAULTS[\"atletico\"] = \"atletico-mg\". Hmm — bare \"Atletico\"... does it appear? Libertadores has \"Athletico\" bare → in aliases → athletico-pr ✓. If \"Atletico\" bare appeared in some dataset, defaulting to atletico-mg is a guess... Copa do Brasil \"Atletico - MG\"? has suffix. I'll keep the default but it's unlikely to fire.\n\nAlso \"Athletico\" bare (Libertadores 2021 final row: 'Athletico') → alias → 'athletico-pr' ✓.\n\nNow — one more critical piece: `_split_tag` requires len(tokens) >= 2. \"SP\" alone as team name? No. OK.\n\nPotential issue: \"ASA AL\" → cleaned \"asa al\" — in aliases? I didn't list 'asa al'. tokens [asa, al] → tag=al, base=\"asa\" unambiguous → strip → \"asa\" ✓. But Copa do Brasil \"A.s.a. - AL\" → cleaned \"a s a al\" → tokens [a, s, a, al] → tag=al → base \"a s a\" → hmm! \"a s a\" → return \"a s a\"?? BAD. The periods in \"A.b.c. - RN\" → \"a b c rn\" → \"a b c\"?? These Copa do Brasil rows have dotted abbreviations: \"A.b.c. - RN\" (= ABC-RN!), \"A.s.a. - AL\" (= ASA-AL). My cleaner splits \"a.b.c.\" into \"a b c\" — no way to know it's \"abc\".\n\nFix: in _clean, remove dots WITHOUT adding spaces? \"A.b.c. - RN\" → \"abc rn\" → tokens [abc, rn] → tag=rn → \"abc\" ✓!! And \"A.s.a. - AL\" → \"asa al\" → \"asa\" ✓. But removing dots without spaces: what about \"Figueirense.FC\"? Doesn't occur. Real names with dots are abbreviations. BUT careful: file names like \"Sada Cruzeiro\"? not here. Let me remove dots without space: replace \".\" with \"\" instead of \" \". Hmm, but \"Bangu-RJ\"? no dots. Numbers with dots? No team names have decimal dots. For dates I don't use _clean. OK: treat '.' as removal (no space).\n\nBut wait — also common in Brazilian datasets: \"C.R. Flamengo\", \"S.E. Palmeiras\", \"A.C.D. ...\" — dot-removal handles these nicely.\n\nHmm, but then \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → parenthetical removed first? My current _clean replaces ALL non-alphanumeric with space, including parens — the parenthetical words REMAIN in the string (\"antigo esporte clube barreira\"). That gives canonical \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira-rj\"?? Ugly but unique-ish. Better: strip parenthetical content BEFORE cleaning. But then \"Nacional (URU)\" loses its tag!! Handle: extract parenthetical content first; if it's a country tag → treat as tag; else drop it. Let me restructure:\n\n```python\ndef normalize_team_id(raw):\n    text = raw.strip()\n    paren = re.findall(r'\\(([^)]*)\\)', text)   # e.g. ['URU'] or ['Minas Gerais'] or ['antigo Esporte Clube Barreira']\n    text_wo_parens = re.sub(r'\\([^)]*\\)', ' ', text)\n    cleaned = _clean(text_wo_parens)   # dots removed, punctuation -> space\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd for FIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\": paren='Minas Gerais' → not a tag → but the FULL alias 'america fc minas gerais' is listed... if I drop parens I lose it! Hmm. So try BOTH: full-string clean INCLUDING parens content for alias lookup, then without. Order:\n1. cleaned_with_parens = _clean(raw) — parens punctuation becomes spaces but words retained: \"america fc minas gerais\" ✓ matches alias!\n2. if in aliases → done. (\"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional uru\" → alias ✓; \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → ✓)\n3. else cleaned_without_parens → continue tag-splitting etc.\n\nThat's the cleanest. \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\": with-parens clean → \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira rj\" — not alias; without-parens → \"boavista sport club rj\" → tag=rj → base \"boavista sport club\" → strip form tokens → \"boavista\" → hmm, then \"boavista\" + ... wait tag handling: base unambiguous → return alias(base) or base... My flow strips the tag for unambiguous → \"boavista\"! But wait — there's \"Boavista-RJ\" (RJ club) and possibly \"Boavista-SC\"? Only RJ one here. OK fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a problem with stripping tags on unambiguous bases... consider \"Coritiba-PR\" vs \"Coritiba\" — both → \"coritiba\" ✓ good. \"Figueirense-SC\"/\"Figueirense\" → \"figueirense\" ✓. This all works.\n\nBUT — what about a club like \"Ceara-CE\" vs... only one Ceará ✓. \"Bahia-BA\" → \"bahia\" ✓ (alias hit anyway).\n\nNow, IMPORTANT trap: what if two different clubs share base in DIFFERENT files where one is suffixed and one bare, and base is ambiguous but spelled differently? E.g. 'Vitoria' bare in BR-Football Serie A 2017 = Vitória-BA ✓ handled by BARE_DEFAULTS. \n\nWhat about 'America' bare? BR-Football has 'america mg' and 'america rn' suffixed, 'america fc natal', 'americano-rj'. Bare 'america'... let me search data for exact bare 'America' — I'll check in the verification step.\n\nOK also: dot removal — careful with decimal-looking team names? None. Fine.\n\nNow let me also double check _clean with dots: \"A.b.c. - RN\" → re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9]+',' ') on \"a.b.c. - rn\" → dots are non-alnum → spaces: \"a b c rn\". DOT REMOVAL must happen before that: first remove '.' chars: \"a.b.c.\" → \"abc\". So: flat = strip_accents(text).casefold(); flat = flat.replace('.', ''); then re.sub. But hyphens must become spaces (\"palmeiras-sp\" → \"palmeiras sp\"). ✓\n\nLet me also handle apostrophes: \"O'Higgins\" → replace with space → \"o higgins\" ✓ fine (or remove? \"o'higgins\" vs \"ohiggins\" — keep space version; consistent within Libertadores file? 'O'Higgins' appears consistently once. fine.)\n\nNow — the remaining question about season determination for BR-Football league games (Jan/Feb → previous year). And 'time' column. And home_goal float parsing ('1.0').\n\nAlso Brasileirao_Matches round column: numbers 1-38. Round display: \"Round 22\" (spec example: \"Brasileirão Round 22\"). For novo: Rodada numbers → \"Round N\". Copa do Brasil round: '1'-'8' → map 8→'Final', 7→'Semifinal', 6→'Quarterfinal', 5→'Round of 16', else 'Round N'. Hmm — actually Copa do Brasil structure: round 1..8. What are rounds 5-8? 5 = oitavas? Let me think: Copa do Brasil 2012-2021: 1st round, 2nd round, 3rd round, round of 16, quarterfinals, semifinals, final — that's 7 stages; the file has 8 rounds. Hmm, counts: r1=334, r2=376, r3=258, r4=140, r5=123, r6=62, r7=30, r8=14. 14 finals-matches over 2012-2021 = 7 two-legged finals? 2012-2018 finals were two-legged (7 seasons × 2 = 14) ✓!! 2019+ single-match finals: 2019, 2020, 2021 — wait 14 total with r8: 2012-2018 = 14 finals ✓ and 2019-2021 finals are in which round? Maybe r7? Hmm r7=30... Let me not over-map; safer: map round number 8→\"Final\", and check 2019/2020/2021 finals — the 2019 Copa do Brasil final was Athletico-PR vs Internacional... The spec example asks \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — I'll check what round the 2019/2020/2021 finals are in and map accordingly. Let me look at r7/r8 rows for 2019-2021 in the verification step.\n\nFor Libertadores: stage column already has 'final', 'semifinals', etc. ✓ display directly.\n\nNow, time to write loader.py with:\n- dataset ids: 'brasileirao', 'novo', 'copa_do_brasil', 'libertadores', 'br_football', 'fifa'\n- per-file row→Match converters\n- primary selection\n- stats attachment\n- players\n\nLet me also define source priority per competition:\n```\nSOURCE_PRIORITY = {\n  SERIE_A: ('brasileirao', 'novo', 'br_football'),\n  SERIE_B: ('br_football',),\n  SERIE_C: ('br_football',),\n  COPA_DO_BRASIL: ('copa_do_brasil', 'br_football'),\n  LIBERTADORES: ('libertadores',),\n}\n```\nPer (comp, season): primary source = first in priority list with matches in that (comp, season).\n\nWait — Série A: 'brasileirao' covers 2012-2022; novo covers 2003-2019. For 2012-2019 brasileirao wins (380 each ✓). 2003-2011 novo ✓. 2023 br_football ✓. \n\nHmm, but consider: does novo 2012-2019 data actually match brasileirao 2012-2019 (same results)? If they disagree (one is wrong), standings differ by source choice. Champions test (2019 Flamengo 90pts) validates.\n\nCopa do Brasil: copa file 2012-2021; br_football 2014-2023. 2012-2013 → copa file only; 2014-2021 → copa file; 2022-2023 → br_football ✓.\n\nEdge: br_football 'Copa do Brasil' season = date year (no Jan tail issue; cup starts Feb/Mar — hmm, wait: are there Copa do Brasil matches in Jan/Feb? 2021 Copa started... Let me check months. If a Feb 2021 match exists, it's the 2021 cup, date-year attribution correct ✓. My league-only rule doesn't touch cup rows ✓.\n\nSérie B/C: Jan/Feb 2021 rows → season 2020 ✓ (COVID tail). 2016-01 single match → 2015 ✓.\n\nNow write the loader.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dda6e1a001Xl216EiJliCYPw","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05dd6ea58001gK6oEKCU7mD8Ex"}}
