s exactly: 17 Vasco (42, survived), 18 Goiás, 19 Coritiba, 20 América Mineiro, and Santos 16th with 43?? \n\nWait — real 2023: Vasco finished 10th? Hmm no. Let me recall... 2023 final: Palmeiras champion 82? The table shows Grêmio 1st with 68 — real 2023: Grêmio finished 2nd with 68? Hmm! Actually yes — I now recall: 2023 final: 1. Palmeiras 82?? or 1. Palmeiras 70, 2. Grêmio 68? Hmm, Grêmio 68 and Palmeiras 70... The real champion 2023 was Palmeiras with 82? No — I'm quite sure Grêmio finished 2nd in 2023 with 68 points and Palmeiras won with 82... no, that gap is too big.\n\nReal 2023 Série A (checking my memory): Palmeiras 82? Let me think about Botafogo: they had 64? Botafogo led with 50+ points at halfway and collapsed to 5th with 64. That matches the data (5. Botafogo 64)! And Grêmio... hmm, real: Grêmio finished 2nd? With 68? And champion Palmeiras with 70? Hmm — the data shows Palmeiras 67 (2nd) and Grêmio 68 (1st).\n\nActually — I remember now with higher confidence: the 2023 champion was Palmeiras with 82 points is definitely wrong. The real: Palmeiras 2023 = 70 points? or 82? Ugh.\n\nLet me look at this from the dataset's perspective: 377 matches (3 short of 380). Missing 3 matches could shift points. Real final 2023 standings (I'm going to trust the general shape): 1. Palmeiras, 2. Grêmio, 3. Atlético-MG, 4. Flamengo, 5. Botafogo... The data has Grêmio 1st by 1 point over Palmeiras. If 3 matches are missing and Palmeiras won one of them (e.g., the delayed match), Palmeiras would be 70 vs 68 — matching the real outcome (Palmeiras 70? Hmm...).\n\nActually YES — I now recall clearly: 2023 Brasileirão — Palmeiras champion with 70 points, Grêmio runners-up with 68. And the title was decided in the final round (Palmeiras beat... hmm). Wait, was it 82? No — 2022 was 81 (38 games). 2023 Palmeiras: 19W-10D-8L = 67 in the data + missing matches. Real 2023: Palmeiras 21W 7D 10L = 70? Hmm...\n\nThe shape (relegations Coritiba/América/Goiás/Santos, Botafogo 5th collapse, Grêmio 2nd) matches reality well. The 377/380 incompleteness explains the Palmeiras/Grêmio inversion. This is a data limitation of the BR-Football 2023 subset — not a code bug. I'll surface \"matches_used\" in standings and add a note in the summary when the season appears incomplete.\n\nI can compute expected matches for a league season: n_teams * (n_teams - 1). For 20 teams → 380. If matches_used < 380, add a caveat to the summary: \"season data incomplete (X of expected Y matches)\". \n\nFor the BDD tests I'll use seasons with complete coverage (2003-2022), not 2023 champion.\n\nNow let me add the incompleteness note to standings, then write the MCP server. Also — I should double check Copa do Brasil primary selection: copa file vs br_football — fractions per season. And Libertadores 2013-2022. Quick check on copa seasons' fractions, plus check that Libertadores final 2019/2020 work.\n\nAlso handle season=None rows (Libertadores 'NA' season, goals '-') — they loaded with season None ✓ and goals None ✓.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dde7f47001QS24T89x54PGyn","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05dde3124001DVoxDMMyhNvTDs"}}
