✓). Then `server.call_tool(...)` — hmm that used the server built from same data...\n\nHmm wait, actually let me re-read the second test output: 'direct service:' worked ✓ (44 matches). Then the call_tool result — I didn't see it because the script crashed at `t.inputSchema` BEFORE calling call_tool! The AttributeError killed the script. So the lowercase output was from the FIRST test only.\n\nFirst test recap: `tools = server.list_tools()` raised TypeError (unawaited coroutine not iterable) — script DIED at line 8. But the output showed the h2h result with lowercase 'fluminense'!? No wait — the first test output shows: TypeError at line 8... and NO h2h print. Right — the h2h lowercase output came from the SECOND test (which had `asyncio.run` with `await` — no wait, the second test output starts with 'tools:' list...\n\nLet me re-trace: Test 2 output was:\n```\ntools: [...] \ncount: 18\n<class 'mcp_types._types.CallToolResult'>\n{...head-to-head Flamengo vs fluminense (0 matches...)}\n```\nTest 3 (just now) output: schema + CORRECT result.\n\nDifference between test 2 and 3: in test 2, I called `server.call_tool('head_to_head', {'team_a': 'Flamengo', 'team_b': 'Fluminense'})` — SAME as test 3. But test 2 gave 'fluminense' + 0 matches. Test 3 gives 'Fluminense' + 44 matches. The only difference: test 3 awaited list_tools() before call_tool. That makes no sense...\n\nUNLESS — mcp tool argument processing is cached/lazy per... hmm. OR — the dict `{'team_a': 'Flamengo', 'team_b': 'Fluminense'}`... in test 2 I passed the same.\n\nWait, actually there's another difference: test 2 called `srv.build_server(data)` AFTER creating svc directly — no, that shouldn't matter.\n\nHmm, honestly — could it be that mcp 2.x call_tool with unawaited prior list_tools coroutine caused weird state? In test 2, `asyncio.run(server.list_tools())` — completed fine in its own loop. Then asyncio.run(call_tool) — hmm.\n\nLet me think about what could produce literally 'fluminense' lowercase in team_b... _resolve('Fluminense') → resolve_team('Fluminense'): lowered='fluminense'; if 'fluminense' in known_teams → return 'Fluminense'. For it to return lowercase, known_teams['fluminense'] would have to BE 'fluminense' — i.e., a team named exactly 'fluminense' (lowercase display) exists in match data! team_display_name(canonical) — for canonical 'fluminense' → CLUB_DISPLAY → 'Fluminense'. But what if a raw team name normalizes to canonical 'fluminense de feira'... no.\n\nOH WAIT!!! I know! It's the FIFA club name! players_by_club keys — FIFA club strings. If FIFA has a club spelled 'Fluminense' → casefold 'fluminense' → known_teams['fluminense'] = 'Fluminense' ✓ same. Fine.\n\nHmm, but what about 'fluminense de feira' (Fluminense de Feira — Bahia club in Série C)? That's a different key.\n\nOK let me just reproduce test 2 exactly and debug what team_b comes through as. Actually — hmm, maybe in test 2, the tool arguments got mangled by the MCP arg validation because... the docstring Args parsing? No.\n\nActually, let me look again at test 2's result text: \"team_b\": \"fluminense\". Curious — let me check: is there any FIFA club 'Fluminense' vs match team... both 'Fluminense'.\n\nYou know what, let me just try to reproduce it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05de00f44001ytasqqIqxx5gZ0","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05ddfcea9001hOUVy739Jyztp7"}}
