{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788281383494,"sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8874a72c1fa04dc1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py","newString":"        self.known_teams: dict[str, str] = {}\n        for team in set(self.matches_by_team):\n            self.known_teams[team.casefold()] = team\n        for club in set(self.players_by_club):\n            self.known_teams.setdefault(club.casefold(), club.title())","oldString":"        self.known_teams: dict[str, str] = {}\n        for team in set(self.matches_by_team) | set(self.players_by_club):\n            self.known_teams[team.casefold()] = team"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py\n@@ -441,10 +441,12 @@\n         if player.nationality:\n             self.players_by_nationality[player.nationality.casefold()].append(player)\n \n     self.known_teams: dict[str, str] = {}\n-    for team in set(self.matches_by_team) | set(self.players_by_club):\n+    for team in set(self.matches_by_team):\n         self.known_teams[team.casefold()] = team\n+    for club in set(self.players_by_club):\n+        self.known_teams.setdefault(club.casefold(), club.title())\n \n # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Lookups\n # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-a725f4ae3f5d/brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py\n@@ -441,10 +441,12 @@\n         if player.nationality:\n             self.players_by_nationality[player.nationality.casefold()].append(player)\n \n     self.known_teams: dict[str, str] = {}\n-    for team in set(self.matches_by_team) | set(self.players_by_club):\n+    for team in set(self.matches_by_team):\n         self.known_teams[team.casefold()] = team\n+    for club in set(self.players_by_club):\n+        self.known_teams.setdefault(club.casefold(), club.title())\n \n # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Lookups\n # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n","additions":3,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py","time":{"start":1788281383491,"end":1788281383493}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Reproduced! When called via `server.call_tool`, team_b resolves to lowercase 'fluminense' with 0 matches. But direct service call works. And earlier (test 3) it worked when... hmm test 3 ALSO showed correct results?! Test 3 output: `\"summary\": \"Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches in dataset)...\"` — wait, no! Test 3 printed the RAW dict from `result.content[0].text` — let me scroll back... Test 3 output ends with:\n\n```\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches in dataset): Flamengo 18 wins, Fluminense 14 wins, 12 draws\",\n```\n\nHmm — that's different from A/B now! Test 3 vs now — what changed? Between test 3 and now I did NOT change any code... wait, did I? No edits between. Test 3: same `asyncio.run(run())` pattern with `await server.list_tools()` first inside same run()... and it worked. Now run2() (same pattern) fails?!\n\nThe difference between test 3's run() and now's run2(): test 3 called list_tools() FIRST then call_tool. Now run2() does the same and FAILS. And test 3's output... hold on, let me re-read test 3's output very carefully:\n\n```\n{\n \"properties\": {...head_to_head schema...}\n}\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches in dataset): Flamengo 18 wins, Fluminense 14 wins, 12 draws\",\n  ...\n```\n\nHmm wait — NO! That was the output of the DIRECT service call `print('direct service:', r['summary'])` — no, that printed 'direct service: Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches...' on one line. The final block printed `result.content[0].text[:300]` — which showed \"Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches...\" — hmm, but in test 3 the script CRASHED at line 18 (`t.inputSchema` AttributeError) BEFORE reaching call_tool!!\n\nSo test 3's final output block... let me look again at test 3 output. It shows:\n1. 'direct service: ...44 matches ✓'\n2. Traceback AttributeError\nWait no — test 3 output was:\n\n```\ndirect service: Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches in dataset): Flamengo 18 wins, Fluminense 14 wins, 12 draws\n\nTraceback ... AttributeError: 'Tool' object has no attribute 'inputSchema'\n```\n\nRight — test 3 CRASHED and never called call_tool. Then TEST 4 (input_schema correct) ran: schema printed, then result.content[0].text[:300] → showed \"Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches...\" — CORRECT!! Hmm wait, test 4's output:\n\n```\n{...schema json...}\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Head-to-head Flamengo vs Fluminense (44 matches in dataset): Flamengo 18 wins, Fluminense 14 wins, 12 draws\",\n  \"team_a\": \"Flamengo\",\n  \"team_b\": \"Fluminense\",\n  \"total_matches\": 44,\n```\n\nSo test 4 (both list_tools AND call_tool awaited in same run) → CORRECT. Now test 5: run() call_tool only → WRONG. run2() list_tools+call_tool same loop → WRONG?!\n\nTest 4 vs test 5 difference... both `asyncio.run` with awaits. Hmm test 4 did `import json` inside the for loop — irrelevant.\n\nOH WAIT. I see it!!! In test 4, the FIRST thing run() did was `tools = await server.list_tools()`, then INSIDE the for loop `print(json.dumps(t.input_schema...))` — for the head_to_head tool. Then `result = await server.call_tool(...)`. CORRECT output.\n\nTest 5 run2(): `tools = await server.list_tools()` then directly `call_tool` — WRONG output. The ONLY difference between test 4 and 5: test 4 imported json and accessed t.input_schema. That's... no effect on behavior.\n\nHmm hold on, is it FLAKY? A race condition — maybe the mcp SDK runs tool calls in a thread pool and the shared SoccerQueryService/SoccerData dict access... my data structures are plain dicts built at load. resolve_team READS known_teams. Thread-safety of dict reads is fine in CPython...\n\nHmm, what about the `run()` calls at module import... no.\n\nWAIT. Let me look at resolve_team again:\n\n```python\ndef resolve_team(self, query):\n    text = query.strip()\n    lowered = text.casefold()\n    if lowered in self.known_teams:\n        return self.known_teams[lowered]\n    canonical = team_display_name(normalize_team_id(text))\n    if canonical.casefold() in self.known_teams:\n        return self.known_teams[canonical.casefold()]\n    for known in sorted(self.known_teams):\n        if known.startswith(lowered):\n            return self.known_teams[known]\n    ...\n```\n\n`known_teams` values: display names. What if TWO entries write to the same key with different values?! `known_teams[team.casefold()] = team` — keys from matches_by_team (display names 'Fluminense') AND players_by_club (FIFA club strings!). If FIFA has a club spelled 'fluminense'... no wait, FIFA spells 'Fluminense'.\n\nHmm, what about 'Flamengo'... resolved 'Flamengo' fine.\n\nOH MY GOD. I think I've got it: `players_by_club` keys are `player.club.casefold()` — LOWERCASED club names! 'fluminense' (lowercase!) is a key in players_by_club! And `known_teams[team.casefold()] = team` stores the ALREADY-CASEFOLDED string as the VALUE for players_by_club keys!! So known_teams['fluminense'] gets overwritten by the players_by_club loop with value 'fluminense' (lowercase, since club keys were casefolded when inserted into players_by_club).\n\nYES — that's the bug:\n\n```python\nfor player in self.players:\n    if player.club:\n        self.players_by_club[player.club.casefold()].append(player)\n...\nfor team in set(self.matches_by_team) | set(self.players_by_club):\n    self.known_teams[team.casefold()] = team\n```\n\nmatches_by_team keys: 'Fluminense' (proper display) → known_teams['fluminense'] = 'Fluminense' ✓.\nplayers_by_club keys: 'fluminense' (casefolded!) → known_teams['fluminense'] = 'fluminense' ✗ OVERWRITES!\n\nIteration order of the union set is arbitrary → sometimes the lowercase wins ('fluminense'), sometimes the proper ('Fluminense'). That explains the flakiness between test runs!\n\nAnd '0 matches': h2h with team 'fluminense' (lowercase) — {m.home_team, m.away_team} == {'Flamengo', 'fluminense'} never matches since match teams are 'Fluminense'.\n\nFix: in _build_indexes, prefer match display names, and don't let casefolded player-club keys override:\n\n```python\nfor team in set(self.matches_by_team):\n    self.known_teams[team.casefold()] = team\nfor team in set(self.players_by_club):\n    self.known_teams.setdefault(team.casefold(), team)\n```\n\nBut also players_by_club keys being casefolded lose original display. For club_squad output I use `players[0].club` — that's the original Player.club ✓ fine.\n\nAlso matches_by_team keys — all display names ✓.\n\nFix known_teams build. Also dedupe: use setdefault so match names win.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05de0676c001PwKYDTGiNB1ZVm","sessionID":"ses_fa22e85f8ffeHYTJ3TNp22RZ2x","messageID":"msg_05de01aae001XTimrV1qewBkFf"}}
